export const EVENT_GUIDANCE = {
    "architectural-design": {
        materials: "The core of this event is a documentation portfolio and a physical scale model. The portfolio is one multipage PDF with student-created drawings (CAD is allowed but not required), a 3D rendering of the final design, a schedule of finish materials, a work log, and a mentorship form signed by an architect or other construction professional. The model sits on a site board whose size is posted with the annual design problem. Foam core is suggested for walls, roof, and site board, and dowels can stand in for columns, but the model cannot include electrical or battery parts, glass, or liquid.",
        difficulty: "You have to think like an architect and show it in a full drawing set that includes a site plan, floor plans, a roof plan, elevations, building sections, and interior views. The portfolio also asks you to explain construction systems such as plumbing, HVAC, and wiring, which is new material for most students. Beginners can learn the drawing conventions, but CAD or drafting experience helps a lot, and the work log and mentor form add paperwork that takes discipline. Judges check that the model matches the drawings, and semifinalists defend their design in a short interview.",
        time: "Most of the work happens before the conference. Teams usually spend weeks on the design concept, the drawing set, the construction systems write-up, and the finish schedule, and they keep a work log as they go because it is a scored portfolio item. The physical model is a separate stage that takes cutting, gluing, and careful scaling, and it has to travel to the conference intact. Semifinalists then prepare a seven minute presentation with two minutes of questions.",
        cost: "Most spending goes into the physical model. Foam core, dowels, adhesive, cutting tools, and a site board are the main purchases, and a larger site or more detailed interiors push the total up. The drawings and 3D rendering can be done with whatever CAD or modeling software your school already provides, so software only costs extra if your school has none. Printing the portfolio photos and presentation materials adds a smaller amount. Reusing scrap foam core and working in a school computer lab keeps costs down.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like architecture, drafting, and turning an idea into a detailed set of plans. It rewards patient, detail-oriented people who enjoy drawing on a computer and building by hand, and who are comfortable explaining design choices to judges. Teams of two to four can enter, and individuals are allowed. Beginners can take it on with sketching and basic CAD practice, but expect a learning curve with construction systems."
    },
    "cad-architecture": {
        materials: "You bring your own computer system to the event with the CAD software already installed. TSA does not provide internet or guarantee hotspot access, so downloaded software is preferred over web-based tools. The required items are one computer and one monitor that can read a USB drive (a laptop is recommended), a USB flash drive for backing up your work, a power strip, pencils, and reference materials on paper only. TSA supplies a table, chair, sketching paper, and electricity.",
        difficulty: "Competitors receive an unseen architectural design problem and produce drawings such as floor plans, elevations, or architectural details in three hours, working alone with no help from teachers, judges, or other participants. The challenge is speed and accuracy in your CAD program combined with knowing how architectural drawings are laid out. Prior CAD practice matters more than anything else here. Beginners can learn enough to compete, but they need regular timed practice.",
        time: "There is no pre-conference submission, so preparation is practice. Most competitors work through timed design problems at home or in a CAD class and build speed with their software's tools. At the event you get 30 minutes to set up, three hours to draw, and a two minute interview, and you should reserve two more hours for the final evaluation process. The rules advise saving your work every fifteen minutes, which is worth rehearsing too.",
        cost: "Costs stay low if you already own a laptop that runs your CAD software. The event itself needs a power strip, a USB drive, pencils, and printed reference materials, which are inexpensive. The bigger question is access to software and a laptop you can bring to the conference, since the rules require you to supply your own. Some schools provide CAD licenses or lab time for practice, and free or education versions of many programs can keep software spending near zero.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who enjoy architectural drafting and computer drawing and like solving problems against a clock. It suits focused, independent people because you compete alone and are interviewed on your own work. It rewards students who already practice in a CAD class. Beginners can compete if they commit to learning one program well."
    },
    "cad-engineering": {
        materials: "You bring your own computer system with the CAD software installed, since TSA does not provide internet or guarantee hotspots. Required items are one computer and one monitor that can read a USB drive (a laptop is recommended), a USB flash drive for backups, a power strip, pencils, and reference materials on paper only. TSA supplies a table, chair, sketching paper, and electricity. The challenge calls for three-dimensional models of subjects such as a machine part, tool, device, or manufactured product, so use a program built for 3D solid modeling.",
        difficulty: "You are handed an unseen engineering problem and must build accurate 3D representations of a part, tool, device, or product within three hours, with no outside help. The difficulty is modeling quickly and correctly, which takes comfort with sketches, dimensions, and how parts fit together. Prior CAD experience helps far more than anything else. Beginners can learn the fundamentals, but they need many timed practice runs before the event.",
        time: "Preparation is mostly practice because there is no pre-conference submission. Competitors typically model sample parts and devices on a timer, learn the tools in their software until they are fast, and practice saving frequently. Onsite you get 30 minutes of setup, three hours to model, a two minute interview, and two more hours reserved for the final evaluation process. Practice happens over several weeks of regular sessions.",
        cost: "That assumes you already have a capable laptop. The required extras are a power strip, a USB drive, pencils, and printed references. The real cost question is software and hardware, because you must bring your own computer, and a laptop that handles 3D modeling smoothly may need decent specifications. Many CAD programs offer free education licenses, and school labs can cover practice time.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like designing parts and mechanisms on a computer and enjoy precise, technical work. It suits people who can stay calm and methodical while solving a problem alone against the clock. Students already taking a CAD or engineering class have a head start, and motivated beginners can build the skills with steady practice."
    },
    "animatronics": {
        materials: "Teams build a complete animatronic device that fits within 15 inches deep, 3 feet wide, and 4 feet high at rest, with a removable outer shell or skin so judges can see the internal components. The rules call for at least three separate movements, plus sound, lights, and sensors, gearing, linkages, or cabling to move the device, and control technology such as a microcontroller running the performance. Fluid power is expected, and leaving it out costs ten points. Wet cell batteries are not allowed, and AC power is only available during your presentation, so plan portable power for testing.",
        difficulty: "Animatronics blends mechanical design, electronics, and programming in one build. The hard parts are getting three coordinated movements to work reliably, integrating fluid power, and keeping the wiring and mechanisms hidden but accessible under a removable shell. Beginners can learn each skill, but prior robotics or electronics experience saves a lot of troubleshooting. Teams also need a short, polished presentation and must answer judges' questions about how the device works.",
        time: "This is a months-long build. Teams usually start by choosing a story that fits the annual theme, then prototype each movement, add the electronics and control program, and finally build the shell and display. Testing and troubleshooting repeat throughout because the device has to run the same way every time. There is no pre-conference submission, but you get only five minutes to set up and five to present, so teams rehearse assembly and the performance many times.",
        cost: "Costs add up through motors or servos, a microcontroller, sensors, lights, a speaker, and the parts for fluid power such as tubing, syringes, or a small air system. The outer shell, display materials, and decorations add more. A school robotics or tech lab can supply tools and some components, and reusing parts from earlier projects or kits reduces spending. Teams that keep the design simple and build the display from inexpensive materials can control the total.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like robotics, electronics, and creative storytelling and enjoy building something that moves and performs. It suits hands-on teams of two or three where people can split mechanical, programming, and design work. Students who are comfortable presenting a working project to judges will enjoy it. Beginners can join, especially with a teammate who has built circuits or robots before."
    },
    "engineering-design": {
        materials: "You need a documentation portfolio saved as a PDF on a USB flash drive, a free-standing display no larger than 15 inches deep, 2.5 feet wide, and 4 feet high, and a tangible prototype or model that fits inside that display space. The display needs a labeled pocket or holder for the USB drive. If anything needs power it must run on dry-cell batteries or photovoltaic cells, and it has to be switched off after setup, with written instructions for judges if they should turn it on. The prototype materials are up to your team and depend on the grand challenge you choose.",
        difficulty: "Teams pick one of the National Academy of Engineering's Grand Challenges that matches the annual theme and take it through the full engineering design process. The portfolio asks for a problem definition, background research, three possible solutions each tied to a scientific or engineering concept, a prototype, and at least four pages describing testing and refinements. The hard part is thinking like an engineer and documenting every step, not just building something. Beginners can compete because the topic is open, but strong research and writing skills matter as much as building skills.",
        time: "Preparation runs for weeks and moves through stages. Teams research the challenge and brainstorm, choose the most plausible of three solutions, build and test a prototype, then revise it and write up each iteration. The portfolio, display, and prototype all have to be ready before check-in. Semifinalists then prepare a ten minute presentation and a five minute question period, so plan rehearsal time too.",
        cost: "Costs depend mostly on the prototype. A small model built from common craft or hardware materials can be inexpensive, while a prototype with electronics or special materials costs more. Beyond that you need a display board, printing for the portfolio and visuals, and a USB drive. The display size limit and the dry-cell or solar power rule keep the build compact, which helps control spending. A school lab may supply tools and workspace.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like open-ended engineering problems that connect to real world issues such as sustainability, health, or security. It suits teams of three or more that can split research, building, testing, and writing. People who enjoy explaining their thinking to judges will do well. It is a good fit for beginners who like to research and prototype but are not ready for a heavy electronics build."
    },
    "structural-design": {
        materials: "Teams bring a pre-built structure made by the team members during the current school year, along with a full-size or scaled three-view drawing (front, top, and right end) and a signed Team Verification form. If you reach the semifinal onsite build, TSA provides the building materials, sketch paper with grids, a pin board, and wax paper, and you bring your own toolbox of up to 20 by 10 by 10 inches. The toolbox must hold what you need, and the guide lists a cutting surface and wax paper as required, with cutting tools that are not electric, adhesives (no aerosol or electric applicators), rulers, abrasives, and marking tools as suggestions. Safety-approved eyewear is required onsite, and prescription glasses need side shields.",
        difficulty: "The event scores structural efficiency, which means holding as much weight as possible for as little structure weight as possible. That takes real understanding of how loads move through a structure, careful building to tight tolerances, and testing to find weak points. The semifinal adds a variation of the problem that you must sketch and build from supplied materials in a three hour window. Beginners can start with simple truss ideas, but experience with building and testing makes a visible difference.",
        time: "Pre-conference work is a cycle of research, design, building, and breaking test structures so you learn how yours fails. Teams commonly build several versions before settling on a final entry, and the final structure must be done before check-in. At the conference the pre-built structure is destructively tested, and 20 teams advance to a three hour onsite build with about 30 minutes for sketching and about two and a half hours to construct. Practicing timed builds with an unfamiliar material set is the best way to prepare for that part.",
        cost: "Most of the cost comes from practice. The structure itself is small and light, and the problem statement posted each year specifies what you may build with, so check it before buying materials. Practice builds are where costs add up, since each test structure is destroyed. You also need hand tools, adhesives, measuring tools, a cutting surface, safety eyewear, and a toolbox. A school shop can often provide tools, and you can reduce expenses by building and testing only a few well-planned iterations.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who love building, testing, and figuring out why things fail. It suits a pair of detail-oriented partners who enjoy precise hand work, measuring, and improving a design through experiments. Students interested in civil or mechanical engineering will find it a good preview. Beginners can enter with patience and a willingness to practice the build."
    },
    "manufacturing-prototype": {
        materials: "Teams make a working prototype that is no larger than 7 by 7 by 7 inches and uses at least two CNC or computer integrated manufacturing processes, such as CNC machining, laser engraving, 3D printing, or CNC vinyl cutting. It must have five major manufactured parts, and plastic, wood, or metal can be used. Stock fasteners and stock electronic components like motors, sensors, and LEDs are allowed. You also need a documentation portfolio with research photos, sketches, an 11 by 17 isometric assembly drawing, detail drawings of each part, real photographs of construction and testing, a bill of materials, and a work log.",
        difficulty: "This event asks you to behave like a small manufacturer. You design a product, draw every part, make at least five parts with CNC or similar machines, and then explain per-unit production cost, materials, and price breaks in a two minute sales pitch. The technical difficulty comes from CAD drawings and from learning to operate or prepare files for machines such as a laser cutter or 3D printer. Beginners can learn it, especially with a shop teacher to guide them, but access to the machines is a big factor.",
        time: "Teams generally spend weeks researching existing solutions, sketching ideas, drawing the parts, and manufacturing and refining the prototype, then documenting construction and testing with real photos. Machine time can be the bottleneck because you may wait for prints or cuts and need to remake parts after testing. The portfolio and prototype are checked in at the conference and then the semifinal pitch is short, so practicing it so it fits in two minutes is essential.",
        cost: "The cost is mostly access to manufacturing equipment and materials. Filament, sheet plastic, wood, or metal stock, fasteners, and any small electronics are the direct purchases, and a school makerspace or technology lab may already provide the machines. If your school lacks a laser cutter or CNC machine, you may need to use outside access, which raises the cost. Printing the portfolio drawings, including the large isometric sheet, adds a smaller amount.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who enjoy design, CAD, and making real products with 3D printers, laser cutters, or CNC machines. It suits a team that likes product design and also wants to practice a business-style sales pitch. Students with access to a school fabrication lab will have an easier time. Beginners can join if they are willing to learn CAD and machine workflows."
    },
    "dragster-design": {
        materials: "You design and build a CO2-powered dragster from a single piece of wood or plastic, including urethane modeling foam. It can be shaped with hand tools, power tools, CNC milling, or 3D printing, but laminated bodies and add-ons such as fenders, canopies, body strengtheners, fiberglass, and shrink wrap are not allowed. You also submit a two-view metric drawing on paper no larger than 11 by 17 inches (hand drafted or CAD) with a one page materials list. The guide sets tight limits on body length, wheelbase, wheels, axle holes, screw eyes, and the CO2 chamber, and the yearly design challenge can change them. TSA provides the CO2 cartridges for the race.",
        difficulty: "Dragster Design is about precision more than raw difficulty. Every dimension has to land inside narrow tolerances, and a car that misses one is disqualified from the race. Beyond that, you are balancing aerodynamics, mass, and friction in the axles and wheels, then explaining your choices in a five minute interview. Beginners can learn the workflow, and the event is individual, so what you build is entirely your own, but careful measuring and finishing skills make the biggest difference.",
        time: "Expect weeks of work. Most competitors begin with design sketches and the technical drawing, then shape the body, drill the axle, screw eye, and CO2 holes, sand and finish it, and check every measurement against the rules. Many students build more than one car so they can refine shape or reduce friction. At the conference your car makes a qualifying run, the top 16 race in a double-elimination bracket, and those builders sit for a short interview.",
        cost: "Most of the money goes into raw materials and hardware. You will buy blocks of wood or foam, plastic wheels, axles, bearings or bushings, screw eyes, and finishing supplies such as sandpaper and paint. A school shop usually provides the saws, drills, and sanders, and some schools have CNC machines or 3D printers that let you skip hand shaping. Building a spare body or two is the main way costs creep up, so measure carefully before cutting.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who enjoy precision building, aerodynamics, and racing. It suits independent, patient people who like shaping something by hand or in CAD and then watching it run. If you enjoy measuring, sanding, and optimizing small details, you will like this event. Beginners are welcome as long as they are willing to follow tolerances exactly."
    },
    "flight-endurance": {
        materials: "You build a rubber-band powered, fixed-wing model aircraft from materials typically used in model building, such as wood, foam, foam board, and plastics. The guide limits the model to a 40 cm wingspan, a fuselage of at least 300 mm, a weight between 8 and 23 grams without the rubber motor, a motor of no more than 1.5 grams, and a fixed-pitch propeller up to 170 mm. The model must be carried in a flight box no larger than 25 by 40 by 60 cm, and you bring safety glasses, a winder (mechanical or battery powered, not AC), and spare motors if you like. You also submit a portfolio with a flight log of your previous ten flights, technical drawings, and a written analysis of your trim adjustments.",
        difficulty: "Making a tiny rubber-powered plane fly for a long time takes patience and a feel for aerodynamics. The challenge is building a frame light enough to stay in the weight range yet strong enough to survive many flights, then adjusting the trim until it flies a consistent circle. The portfolio adds a technical review of your flight log that explains why each change helped. Beginners can learn from simple designs, and prior model aircraft experience saves a lot of trial and error.",
        time: "Preparation is a long loop of building, test flying, and adjusting. Most of the time goes into trimming, because each change to wing position, ballast, or propeller affects how long the model stays up, and the guide expects you to log your flights. You also need time for the drawings and the analysis in the portfolio. At the conference you get 30 minutes to trim and two official flights, so practicing in a space with a similar ceiling height helps.",
        cost: "The aircraft itself uses small amounts of material. Balsa or foam, rubber motor material, a propeller, small wire parts, and wheels cover the aircraft itself, and a winder is a small extra. Since delicate models break, building several airframes is the main expense, along with a flight box for transport. You may need to find a gym or similar indoor space for practice, which is usually free through a school.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who enjoy model aircraft, aerodynamics, and fine hands-on work. It rewards people who can stay patient through lots of small adjustments and who like keeping careful records. Because it is an individual event, it suits self-motivated builders. Beginners can compete if they are willing to practice flying and tweaking the model."
    },
    "transportation-modeling": {
        materials: "You build a scale model of a vehicle that fits the annual design problem, and you present it on a display no larger than 16 by 16 by 16 inches, including the model. The model must be original work made during the current school year from wood, urethane modeling foam, or 3D printing, and it must measure at least six inches long. Store-bought model body parts are prohibited, but pre-manufactured items such as wheels, tires, canopies, mirrors, lights, and antennae are allowed. You also need a documentation portfolio in a clear front report cover with research, concept drawings, orthographic illustrations on 11 by 17 paper, construction photos, and MLA citations. TSA provides no electrical access for displays.",
        difficulty: "The challenge is making a convincing, realistic vehicle model by hand that also meets the annual problem. That takes skill in shaping foam or wood or in 3D modeling, plus finishing work such as sanding, painting, and fitting small parts. The portfolio asks for research into the vehicle's history and design drawings, and semifinalists answer questions from judges about their research and process. Beginners can do well if they plan carefully, but the polish expected in finished models rewards experience.",
        time: "The work stretches over weeks. Typical stages are researching the vehicle, sketching or modeling concepts, building a mock-up, carving or printing the final body, finishing it, and documenting the production with photos. The portfolio drawings and write-up take their own time, so many students work on them alongside the model. Semifinalists spend a few more days preparing for a five minute interview.",
        cost: "Costs depend on how you build the body. Wood blocks, modeling foam, or filament for 3D printing, paint and finishing supplies, and small purchased parts like wheels and lights are the main expenses. A 3D printer or CNC machine at school can save money on materials and speed up the work. Printing the portfolio, especially the large 11 by 17 pages, and making a display add smaller costs.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who love cars, aircraft, or boats and enjoy sculpting and finishing detailed models. It suits independent, patient people with an eye for design and realism. Students who like both hands-on making and researching the history of a vehicle will enjoy the portfolio too. Beginners can enter, especially with a plan and some practice shaping materials."
    },
    "drone-challenge": {
        materials: "Teams assemble an open-source four-motor drone with propellers between 4 and 8 inches, propeller guards, and a camera for the pilot, and the current guide has the drone use magnets or grippers for the yearly theme. It can be built from a kit and modified, with a frame made from plastic, wood, or 3D-printed material, and commercially available ready-made drones are not allowed. Battery packs must be commercially available lithium-ion or lithium polymer packs. For the pit area, bring two welding blankets of at least 4 by 6 feet, chargers, a toolbox, spare parts, a power strip, an extension cord, and a laptop that runs on battery power. Teams also submit a PDF portfolio with a photo log, wiring schematics, a software explanation, a bill of materials, drawings, and research on drone flight regulations.",
        difficulty: "This event combines building, wiring, programming, and flying. You need to understand flight controllers, speed controllers, motors, and batteries, configure flight software for stable flight, and then pilot the drone through the yearly challenge on a ten minute clock. Safety rules are strict, and breaking them can cost points. Beginners can learn the pieces with a kit and a mentor, but hands-on experience with electronics or RC aircraft is a big advantage.",
        time: "Teams generally spend months. They plan the build, assemble and wire the frame, configure the software, and then practice flying and fixing problems, since crashes and part swaps are common. The portfolio takes real time too because it documents each build step, wiring, software, and legal research. At the conference there are 10 minutes to set up, a 30 minute test session with a safety check, a ten minute challenge run, and a ten minute interview for semifinalists.",
        cost: "The electronics are the main expense. A drone kit or separate frame, four motors, speed controllers, a flight controller, a radio controller, a camera and video transmitter, and several batteries add up quickly, and crashes mean replacement parts. Chargers, welding blankets, safety glasses, and safety vests are further purchases. Schools with an existing robotics or drone program may lend a radio or tools, and a team can lower cost by starting with a simple kit.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who enjoy electronics, flying, and troubleshooting hardware. It suits teams of two to six that can divide mechanical building, wiring, software setup, piloting, and documentation. It is a strong fit for anyone curious about aerospace or robotics, as long as they are comfortable with careful safety procedures. Beginners can join, but a teammate with RC or electronics experience helps."
    },
    "robotics": {
        materials: "Teams build an open-source robot that meets the yearly challenge, starting from a commercial kit or from separate parts. The frame can be plastic, wood, or 3D-printed material, batteries must be commercially available lithium-ion or lithium polymer packs, and the robot must fit in a 16 inch cube at the start and have removable covers for inspection. Required pit gear includes two welding blankets of at least 4 by 6 feet, a radio controller, chargers, a toolbox, spare parts, a power strip, an extension cord, and the computers and software you need. The drive method is up to you, and legs, tracks, or wheels are all allowed, and a camera is optional.",
        difficulty: "Robotics asks a team to build, wire, program, and drive a robot through a level course under a ten minute clock. You need to choose a drive system that works on whatever surface the venue has, keep the electronics reliable, and drive accurately, all while following strict battery and pit safety rules that carry point deductions. Beginners can start from a kit, but experience with building and debugging robots helps a lot. Semifinalists also face a ten minute interview about their design decisions.",
        time: "Robotics is a months-long project. Teams commonly plan the design around the yearly challenge, build and wire a first version, write and tune the control code, and then spend many practice sessions driving the course and fixing what breaks. Spare parts and rebuilds are part of the process. At the conference the schedule is 10 minutes to set up the pit, a 30 minute testing and safety session, the ten minute challenge run, and an interview for semifinalists.",
        cost: "Costs rise quickly because a robot needs a kit or frame, motors, a controller, batteries and chargers, a radio, and plenty of spare parts. You also pay for pit equipment such as welding blankets and for materials to rebuild after crashes. Schools with an existing robotics program often supply a kit, tools, or controllers, which can cut a team's out-of-pocket spending. Starting from a simple kit and buying spares only as needed keeps the total manageable.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who love building, wiring, and programming robots and enjoy fast-paced problem solving. It suits teams of two to six that can share mechanical design, electronics, coding, and driving. People who like testing, breaking, and improving a machine will do well. Beginners can join with a kit and a teammate who knows basic electronics or coding."
    },
    "biotechnology-design": {
        materials: "The main deliverables are an interactive display, a documentation portfolio, and a multimedia presentation. The display can be no larger than 15 inches deep, 2.5 feet wide, and 4 feet high, including the portfolio, and a model or prototype is optional. AC power is not allowed, so any lights or electronics must use dry-cell or photovoltaic power, and no harmful substances, live plants, or animals can be displayed. Semifinalists present the multimedia on a battery-powered laptop that the team supplies, and projectors are not permitted.",
        difficulty: "The challenge is understanding a contemporary biotechnology topic well enough to explain its science, benefits, risks, and ethical questions to a general audience. The display has to look like it belongs in a children's museum for the year's audience, which means turning technical ideas into something clear and engaging. Beginners can handle it with careful research, but strong writing and science communication skills matter. In the interview, judges can probe how well you actually understand the topic.",
        time: "Most of the time goes into research and communication. Teams typically spend weeks reading sources, deciding on a solution to present, writing the portfolio sections, and designing the display and slides. An optional model adds build time. Semifinalists then practice a seven minute presentation with two minutes of questions, so rehearsal matters.",
        cost: "Costs are mostly the display board, printing, and any model materials. Printing the portfolio and slide handouts adds to the total. A prototype or model is optional, so teams that skip it can keep spending low. Battery-powered lights or small electronics add a little more, and a laptop is usually already available.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who are curious about biology, medicine, or biotechnology and like explaining science in a clear, interesting way. It suits teams that enjoy research and design more than heavy building. Students who like public speaking and interviews will do well. Beginners can start with a topic they care about and learn the science as they go."
    },
    "board-game-design": {
        materials: "Your team designs, builds, and packages an entirely original board game, including the board, pieces, cards, and printed instructions. The packaged game can be no larger than 12 by 18 by 3 inches, and the team chooses its own materials for the game and packaging. Dice and sand timers may be commercially made, but everything else must be the team's own work or properly documented with copyright permission. The instructions go both in the box and in a PDF portfolio on a USB drive, along with engineering drawings, a cost summary, a work log, and a copyright checklist.",
        difficulty: "The hard part is designing a game that is actually fun, balanced, and clear to a new player, not just one that looks good. Judges expect polished packaging, instructions that explain every rule in detail, and a game that can be set up within five minutes of opening the box and repackaged within five minutes afterward. Beginners can compete, but game design takes real playtesting, and the portfolio adds documentation work. In the semifinal you demonstrate the game and answer questions about your design process.",
        time: "Expect a multi-week project that moves through concept, prototype, playtesting, and final production. Teams usually test the rules with friends several times and revise them, then build the final board, pieces, and box and write clear instructions. The portfolio has to be assembled along the way, and the semifinal asks for a quick setup, a seven minute demonstration, and a repackage, so timing needs practice.",
        cost: "Costs depend on how finished you want the components to be. Printing the board and cards, sturdy card stock or foam board, game pieces, and a box or other packaging are the main purchases. Fancier parts such as custom tokens or professional-looking printing push the price up, while handmade pieces keep it down. A school printer or craft supplies can cover a lot, and the required cost summary in the portfolio is a good way to plan the budget.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who love tabletop games and enjoy designing rules, art, and packaging. It suits creative teams that like playtesting, fixing problems, and explaining their design to others. People who are comfortable demonstrating a game in front of judges will enjoy the semifinal. Beginners are welcome if they like to experiment and revise."
    },
    "promotional-design": {
        materials: "You make a student-designed two-pocket cardstock folder with a cover image and four printed promotional items of your choice, such as a pamphlet, postcard, letter, or business card. Each item is designed on paper no larger than 8.5 by 11 inches, and templates and pre-printed paper are not allowed, though clip art is permitted if you document it. The entry and portfolio go in a standard 10 by 13 inch envelope. If you advance, you bring your own computer with a USB port and design software for an onsite design problem, since TSA does not provide internet access.",
        difficulty: "This is a graphic design event, so success depends on layout, typography, color, and visual consistency across all pieces. The folder and the four items must share a unified design and respond to the annual theme, and any template use leads to disqualification. The semifinal is a two hour onsite design challenge done on your own computer, so you need to work quickly in your design software. Beginners can learn the tools, but solid graphic design practice is an advantage.",
        time: "Most of the preliminary work happens over several weeks. You interpret the theme, plan the visual identity, design and print the four items, build the folder, and write the portfolio that explains your choices and the software used. If you reach the semifinal, there is a 30 minute setup and a two hour on-site challenge, so practicing timed design tasks before the conference is useful.",
        cost: "Costs are modest and mostly cover printing and paper. You need cardstock for the folder, printed copies of the four items, and an envelope, and color printing adds to the total if your school does not provide it. Design software is the other factor, since free tools exist but your school may already provide professional programs. You also need a laptop for the semifinal.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who enjoy graphic design, branding, and making polished printed pieces. It suits creative, detail-oriented people who like working alone and responding to a theme. It is a good match for students who already use design software and want to practice working under a time limit. Beginners can try it if they are willing to learn layout basics."
    },
    "childrens-stories": {
        materials: "You create an original illustrated storybook that closes to no more than 12 by 12 inches and tells a story of no more than 1,500 words with at least seven original illustrations. Illustrations must be freehand or computer-generated from basic lines and shapes by team members, with no templates, previously existing characters, or copyrighted material. The book must be bound by the team, with no professional binding. You also need a documentation portfolio with photos of the construction process, a work log, and summaries of at least two field tests in which you read the book to children in the target age range. Art supplies, paper, and binding materials are your own choice.",
        difficulty: "This event asks you to do several things well at once, including writing a story, creating original art, designing a physical book, and testing it with real children. The most demanding parts are drawing every illustration yourself, keeping the word count within the limit, and building a sturdy, attractive book by hand. Beginners can compete, since the story can be simple, but the field tests and portfolio require planning ahead. Semifinalists present their book and answer questions about the writing, illustration, and construction.",
        time: "This is a multi-week project. Teams often draft and edit the story first, then sketch and finish the illustrations, assemble the book, and run at least two field tests that need to be scheduled with an audience of children. Documenting construction with photos and writing the research and project summaries takes extra time. Semifinalists then prepare a ten minute presentation and a five minute interview.",
        cost: "Costs mostly cover art and book-making supplies. Paper or cardstock, drawing or painting materials, binding supplies, and printing are the main purchases, and a pop-up or interactive format needs a few extra materials. If you draw digitally, a tablet or software may add cost, but many teams use tools they already own. Printing the portfolio is another small expense.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like writing, drawing, and crafting, and who enjoy working with kids. It suits creative teams or individuals who want to see their ideas turned into a physical book. People who are comfortable reading and presenting to children will find the field tests rewarding. Beginners can do well with a short, clear story and careful craftsmanship."
    },
    "data-science": {
        materials: "The main tools are a dataset and the software you use to analyze it, which the rules leave open (spreadsheets such as Excel or code files both count). Your data must include at least 500 rows, and your portfolio PDF includes a data dictionary, methods, results, conclusions, an appendix of your raw analysis, and a digital scientific poster built from an optional TSA template. If you reach the semifinal you bring a laptop with a USB converter if needed, and no electricity or internet is provided. Generative AI tools are not allowed.",
        difficulty: "You have to collect or compile real data, clean and analyze it, make predictions, and explain what it means for society. The semifinal raises the stakes with a surprise data set that you must visualize and summarize in two hours. Beginners can start with spreadsheets and basic charts, but comfort with statistics, graphing, and a tool such as Excel or a programming language gives you a strong advantage. You also have to present the poster clearly to judges.",
        time: "The portfolio PDF is due in mid-May, so teams spend weeks finding a dataset tied to the annual theme, cleaning it, analyzing it, building charts, and writing the sections. The preliminary round adds a ten minute poster presentation with six minutes to present. Semifinalists then complete a two hour onsite data visualization challenge, so practicing quick analysis on unfamiliar data is the best preparation.",
        cost: "This event is inexpensive because most of the work is digital. Data from public sources is free, and spreadsheets or free programming tools can handle the analysis. Your main requirements are a laptop that runs on battery power and a way to print or save the portfolio. If you want to use paid software, check whether your school already has a license.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like numbers, patterns, and turning data into a story. It suits analytical teams that enjoy research, graphs, and explaining their conclusions. Students with some experience in statistics, coding, or spreadsheets will have an edge, but motivated beginners can learn on the job. It is a good match for anyone curious about careers in analytics or research."
    },
    "software-development": {
        materials: "Everything you need is a computer and the programming tools of your choice. The rules do not require a specific language, but your team must submit the executable code online before the deadline, either as a link to a repository such as GitHub or as a link to a PDF of all of the code. For the presentation you bring your own hardware, such as a laptop on battery power or mobile devices, with a setup that fits in a 2 foot cube. TSA does not provide wireless internet, so teams are advised to have a backup plan if a hotspot fails.",
        difficulty: "Teams identify a societal need based on the yearly theme and build a working software tool, such as a dashboard, app, or predictive model. Judges look at technical skill, how well the tool solves the problem, and whether you can explain your own code when questioned. The difficulty depends on your chosen project, but you need real programming experience to build something functional. Beginners can contribute, but the team should include at least one confident programmer.",
        time: "This is a project-based event. Teams usually spend weeks planning the idea, designing and coding the tool, testing it, and documenting the design process. The code is due in mid-May, and the presentation is a short seven minutes plus three minutes of questions, so a lot of time also goes into preparing a smooth demo that works without relying on the internet.",
        cost: "Costs are low because software development uses tools most students already have. Free programming languages and code repositories cover most needs. You might spend on hosting, an API, or a device for the demo, but none of these is required. A laptop that can run your project without a power outlet is the main hardware need.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who enjoy programming and building apps or tools that help people. It suits teams that like planning a project, coding it together, and showing it off. Anyone interested in computer science, AI, or app design will find it rewarding. Beginners can learn alongside experienced teammates, but the project does need real coding."
    },
    "webmaster": {
        materials: "You build and publish a website that anyone can reach online at all times, with at least three pages linked from the main solution page and no password required. A reference page must list your sources and link to a completed Student Copyright Checklist and Work Log as PDFs. You can write plain HTML and CSS, use JavaScript libraries, or build on a framework such as WordPress or Bootstrap, but pre-built templates and themes are not allowed. The site has to work in current versions of major browsers, so you need a web host, which can be free or paid.",
        difficulty: "You need to design, code, and launch a site that responds to the yearly topic. Judges review the live website and then ask semifinalists about the design methods and the research behind the content. The hard parts are building everything from scratch without a template and making the site look polished and work well. Beginners with basic HTML and CSS can compete, but experience with layout and JavaScript helps.",
        time: "Most teams spend weeks designing, coding, testing, and refining the site before the mid-May submission deadline, because changes after the deadline are not judged. Time also goes into researching the topic and writing the page content. If you advance, you prepare for a ten minute interview about your design choices and process.",
        cost: "This event is inexpensive because most of the work is coding. Free code editors and many free hosting options are available. A custom domain or paid hosting is optional. Most teams use computers their school already provides.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like web design, coding, and visual problem solving. It suits teams that enjoy building something people can actually visit online. Students interested in web development or UX will gain real skills. Beginners can start with HTML and CSS and grow from there."
    },
    "video-game-design": {
        materials: "You need a game engine or coding tools of your choice, a place to host the finished game online, and a screen recorder for the demonstration video. The game has to run in a browser from a direct link, because entries that require a download or an executable file are not judged, and it must be playable by judges through at least the third level. You also submit a demonstration video under five minutes that shows a tutorial, at least one level, and the code, scripts, files, and game engine behind it, plus a PDF portfolio with a hand-drawn storyboard, a copyright checklist, and a work log. Generative AI tools are not allowed, and the game must meet an ESRB rating of E for Everyone.",
        difficulty: "Building a complete, polished game that plays for more than three minutes and works on any browser is a big task. You need programming skills, level design, art and sound, and enough testing that judges can reach the third level without bugs. The demo video and storyboard add production and planning work, and the interview tests how well you understand your own project. Beginners can learn a beginner-friendly engine, but a team with real coding and art skills has a big advantage.",
        time: "Expect a long project that spans weeks or months. Teams plan the concept and storyboard, build and test levels, add art and audio, host the game, record the demo video, and finish the portfolio before the mid-May online submission deadline. The game must stay playable until the end of the conference, so you cannot change it after submission. Semifinalists then prepare for a ten minute interview.",
        cost: "Costs are low because most game engines and hosting platforms have free options. You may pay for assets, but anything not made by your team needs documented permission, so many teams create their own art and sound. A computer and screen recording software are the main needs. Paid engines, assets, or hosting are optional.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who love games and want to build one from scratch, combining coding, art, music, and storytelling. It suits teams where members can split programming, design, and testing. It is a strong fit for anyone considering a career in game development or software. Beginners can join with a friendly engine, but should plan on a steep learning curve."
    },
    "vr-simulation": {
        materials: "You create a two to three minute visualization using video and 3D computer graphics tools, and the rules suggest programs such as Blender, Unity, Maya, 3DS Max, and Adobe Animate. The entry is delivered as an MPEG file on a USB flash drive inside a portfolio that also has a hand-sketched storyboard, a software and hardware list, references, a work log, and a copyright checklist. Purchased content such as textures, models, or royalty-free music is not allowed, and neither are PowerPoint slideshows, live action video, or web apps like Animaker and Powtoons. Sound must accompany the simulation, and semifinalists present it on virtual reality hardware that the team supplies.",
        difficulty: "The challenge is building original 3D scenes and animation from scratch, with no purchased assets, that explain a scientific or technical idea in exactly two to three minutes. Missing the time window costs points, and the work must advance on its own without anyone clicking through. Learning a 3D tool such as Blender takes time, and storyboarding every shot and sound cue adds planning work. Beginners can learn the basics, but 3D modeling experience helps a great deal.",
        time: "Most of the work is modeling, animating, and rendering, and those steps take a lot of repetition. Teams usually storyboard first, then build assets, assemble scenes, add sound, and export and test the final file. Rendering and fixing problems can eat up time, so starting early matters. Semifinalists also prepare a short presentation and a four minute interview on their own VR equipment.",
        cost: "Many 3D programs, including free ones, can handle the project, so software does not have to cost anything. Because purchased assets are banned, you spend time instead of money. The main cost question is hardware, since 3D rendering needs a capable computer and semifinalists must bring VR gear, which a school may be able to lend.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who enjoy 3D modeling, animation, and visual storytelling and like explaining science with images. It suits creative people who are comfortable learning technical tools like Blender or Unity. It works for individuals or small teams. Beginners can start with simple scenes, but plan to spend time learning the software."
    },
    "hs-audio-podcasting": {
        materials: "You need a way to record voices, audio editing software, and somewhere to host the finished MP3 so judges can open a direct link without installing anything. The podcast must be more than one minute and less than five, and it can include free, non-copyrighted sounds, loops, or music as long as you describe them in the portfolio. You also submit a PDF portfolio with a timeline showing every voice, a list of hardware and software, a work log, a copyright checklist, and square podcast cover art at 1400 by 1400 pixels. Semifinalists produce new podcasts onsite during a 36 hour window, so they need a laptop and their recording and editing setup at the conference.",
        difficulty: "The skill is storytelling with sound. Your piece has to hold attention for up to five minutes using writing, voice acting, and folly sound effects, and timing errors cost points in 15 second increments. Editing multiple voices cleanly takes practice, and the semifinal tests whether you can script, record, and produce a new piece in about 36 hours with requirements revealed at the orientation meeting. Beginners can learn free editing tools quickly, but clean audio and good pacing come from practice.",
        time: "Preliminary work is a few weeks of scripting, recording, editing, and building the portfolio before the mid-May online deadline. Teams usually go through several recording sessions and edits because small mistakes in audio stand out. The semifinal is a short, intense production sprint, so rehearsing a quick script-record-edit workflow beforehand pays off.",
        cost: "Cost depends mostly on your recording setup. A smartphone or basic USB microphone can work, and free audio editors are widely available. Paid editing software, a better microphone, or headphones are optional upgrades. The bigger factor is access to a quiet place to record, since echo and background noise are hard to fix in editing, and schools can sometimes provide a quiet room or sound-dampened space.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who enjoy storytelling, voice acting, sound design, or radio-style content. It suits creative teams that can write a script, perform it, and edit with care, and solo entries are allowed too. Students interested in media, journalism, or audio engineering will enjoy it. Beginners can join, especially if they like experimenting with free editing tools."
    },
    "music-production": {
        materials: "You need a computer with music production software and a way to record or create original sounds, such as instruments, vocals, or synthesized tracks. The finished piece must be more than one minute and less than three, hosted as an MP3 or similar file at a direct link. Free, non-copyrighted loops and sounds are allowed if you document them. Your PDF portfolio includes a timeline of every instrument, voice, and synthesized track, a work log, a self-evaluation, and notes on any outside elements.",
        difficulty: "You are producing an original song that fits the annual theme and sounds finished, which takes musical ideas plus technical mixing skills. Layering tracks, balancing levels, and keeping the length within the time window are the main hurdles, and each 15 seconds outside the limit costs points. The portfolio asks you to explain how the piece was built track by track. Beginners can start with a simple arrangement in a free program, but music theory and production experience help.",
        time: "Preparation takes weeks. Teams usually write and arrange the piece, record or program each track, mix and revise it, and then document the process in the portfolio before the mid-May deadline. Revisions and mixing can take longer than expected. Semifinalists prepare for a ten minute interview about how they made the music.",
        cost: "This event can stay inexpensive since many music production programs have free versions and a computer is all you need to program sounds. Costs rise if you want a better microphone, an audio interface, MIDI keyboard, or paid software. Using instruments or a school music room you already have access to reduces spending.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who write, play, or produce music and want to build a complete track with a story behind it. It suits creative teams or solo producers who enjoy experimenting with sound. Students interested in audio engineering or composing will find it rewarding. Beginners can start with a simple loop-based piece."
    },
    "digital-video-production": {
        materials: "You need a camera or smartphone, a way to capture sound, and video editing software, plus a place to host the finished video online with a direct link. The video must be no longer than three minutes, and all footage, graphics, special effects, and audio must be originally created by your team. Your PDF portfolio includes a hand-sketched storyboard (screenshots are not accepted), a script, a list of hardware and software, permission letters for any copyrighted material, a copyright checklist, signed consent forms for everyone who appears on camera, and a work log.",
        difficulty: "The challenge is telling a clear story in under three minutes with footage you shot yourself. Judges look at planning, camera work, editing, sound, and how well the video matches the theme, and anything borrowed has to be properly cited or permitted. The portfolio adds a storyboard, script, and consent paperwork. Beginners can learn basic editing, but good lighting, sound, and pacing take practice.",
        time: "Expect weeks of work. Teams usually write the script and storyboard first, then film, then edit, and often reshoot scenes after reviewing the cut. Collecting signed consent forms and writing the portfolio also takes time. The video and PDF are due online in mid-May, and semifinalists have a ten minute interview.",
        cost: "Costs are modest if you already have a smartphone or school camera. Free or low-cost editing software can cover the editing. Better lighting, microphones, or tripods are optional upgrades, and props or locations may add small costs. A school media lab can often lend equipment.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like filming, editing, and storytelling. It suits creative teams that can split writing, directing, camera work, and editing. Students interested in film, media, or marketing will gain useful skills. Beginners can start with a simple, well-planned story."
    },
    "on-demand-video": {
        materials: "Your team brings its own video camera (no more than one may be used) and its own editing equipment, since the whole film is shot and edited at the conference. Editing must be done on a nonlinear editing system or on your camera. You also need a way to post the finished video online with a direct link, plus a completed Student Copyright Checklist saved as a PDF. TSA does not provide wireless internet, so plan for uploading with your own connection. Props and a line of dialogue are revealed at the event, and everything must be filmed at officially approved conference locations.",
        difficulty: "The challenge is speed and planning. You have 36 hours from the orientation meeting to write, film, edit, and submit a video no longer than 60 seconds, and anything over a minute is disqualified. You have to work with required props and dialogue you have never seen, and you cannot film in places such as restrooms, restaurants, or elevators. Beginners can compete if they understand basic filming and editing, but teams that practice a fast workflow have a big advantage.",
        time: "There is no pre-conference submission, so preparation is practice. Teams usually run mock challenges to build a routine for brainstorming, shooting, and editing a 60 second story quickly. At the conference the 36 hour clock starts at the orientation meeting, and teams have to balance filming, editing, uploading, and the rest of their conference schedule.",
        cost: "Costs are mostly equipment you may already have. A camera or smartphone, a laptop with editing software, and a tripod or microphone cover the basics, and free editing programs are widely available. Travel to the conference is the larger expense, and equipment can sometimes be borrowed from a school media lab.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like making films quickly and thinking on their feet. It suits teams that can divide directing, acting, filming, and editing under pressure. Students interested in video production or storytelling will enjoy the creative constraints. Beginners can join, but should practice timed shoots first."
    },
    "stem-mass-media": {
        materials: "You need a camera, a way to capture clear audio, and video editing software to produce a news broadcast of no more than four minutes. All audio and visuals must be produced by your team, though royalty-free images, video, and music are allowed if cited and listed on the Student Copyright Checklist. Your PDF portfolio includes the checklist, MLA-style references, and consent forms for anyone identifiable on camera. Semifinalists record audio only during a press conference and then write a news story of up to 500 words and submit it as a PDF.",
        difficulty: "You report on a scientific headline chosen each year, explain its future implications, and present it like a real news segment. That takes strong writing, on-camera delivery, and editing. The semifinal adds a different skill, which is preparing interview questions in ten minutes and then interviewing a professional in a 30 minute press conference before writing a news story. Beginners can learn the basics, but confidence in speaking and writing under pressure matters.",
        time: "Prep is a few weeks of research, scriptwriting, filming, and editing the video before the mid-May online deadline. Teams often rehearse on camera several times to improve delivery. For the semifinal, the best preparation is practicing interviews and quick article writing, since the briefing, press conference, and writing all happen under time limits.",
        cost: "Costs are low because a smartphone or school camera, free editing tools, and a quiet space are enough. A microphone, simple lighting, or a tripod improves quality but is optional. A voice recorder for the semifinal press conference is a small purchase if you do not already have a phone that records audio.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who enjoy journalism, news presenting, and explaining science to a wide audience. It suits teams of two or three who like writing, speaking on camera, and asking good questions. Students interested in communications or media will get strong practice. Beginners can join if they are comfortable speaking in front of a camera."
    },
    "photographic-technology": {
        materials: "You need a camera, since your portfolio must report details such as f-stop, exposure time, ISO, and focal length for each image, and photo editing software. Cell phone cameras are not permitted for the semifinal. The preliminary portfolio is a PDF with five images, one color, one black and white, one macro, one still life, and one of your choice, each with a written statement. Semifinalists bring a tripod, an optional off-camera flash, a laptop with graphics editing software, a media reader, and a USB drive that TSA keeps. No internet or power is provided onsite.",
        difficulty: "You must understand camera settings, composition, lighting, and editing well enough to explain each decision in writing. The assignment covers different styles such as macro and still life, and the semifinal gives you 24 hours to shoot and edit a new set of photos that you then present in five minutes and discuss in a five minute interview. Beginners can learn the exposure basics, but real camera experience makes a big difference.",
        time: "Preliminary work takes a few weeks because teams plan the theme, shoot and reshoot, edit the images, and write the statements before the mid-May deadline. The semifinal then compresses everything into 24 hours of shooting, editing, and building a portfolio. Practicing a timed shoot-edit workflow helps most.",
        cost: "Cost depends on the camera. A camera is the biggest need, and many schools can lend one. A tripod, a flash, and editing software are the other items, and free editing programs exist. Printing is not required because the portfolio is a PDF.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who love photography and want to understand the technical side of it. It suits independent, creative people who enjoy planning shots and perfecting edits. Students interested in visual arts or media will build useful skills. Beginners can join if they are willing to learn camera settings."
    },
    "technology-bowl": {
        materials: "There is nothing to build or submit. In the preliminary round each of the three team members takes a one hour objective test, either online or on a scan sheet, and the team ranking comes from the average of their scores. If it is a scan-sheet test, bring two sharpened No. 2 pencils. For the semifinal, teams answer questions in head-to-head oral rounds, and transmitting or recording devices are not allowed in the event area.",
        difficulty: "The event covers TSA knowledge and a broad range of technology content standards, so the challenge is breadth of knowledge and quick recall. The written test rewards steady study across many topics, and the semifinal adds buzzer-style questions with five second answer windows and point penalties for wrong answers. Beginners can do well if they study consistently, but teams with strong general technology knowledge have an advantage. Everyone on the team must be ready, since the test average and the oral rounds both depend on all three members.",
        time: "Preparation is mostly studying and practice quizzing over weeks. Teams often review TSA facts and technology topics, take practice tests, and run mock buzzer rounds to build speed and teamwork. There is no pre-conference submission. At the conference the written test takes one hour, and teams that advance spend additional time on the oral competition.",
        cost: "This is one of the least expensive events because it requires no equipment or building. The only items you need are pencils for a scan-sheet test and study materials, which are often free.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who enjoy trivia, quiz competitions, and learning about many technology topics. It suits teams of three that can study together and stay calm under buzzer pressure. People who like fast recall and teamwork will enjoy the oral rounds. Beginners can join and build knowledge as they practice."
    },
    "technology-problem-solving": {
        materials: "TSA provides the problem and the materials onsite, and you may use only what is issued to you, except glue and masking tape from your own toolbox. You must bring a toolbox or container no larger than 20 by 10 by 10 inches with your school name, address, and advisor cell number on it, and teams without one cannot compete. The guide requires a cutting surface that protects the table and safety glasses, and it suggests non-electric cutting tools, adhesives (no aerosol or electric applicators), temporary fasteners, rulers, marking tools, and a sheet of wax paper. Another team checks your toolbox against a verification sheet, and sharing tools is not allowed.",
        difficulty: "You get an unseen problem and 90 minutes to design and build a solution that is judged by an objective measure such as time, distance, or strength. The difficulty lies in quick planning and careful, precise building with limited materials and tools. Nobody can predict the exact problem, so success comes from broad building skills and good teamwork between the two partners. Beginners can learn from practice challenges, but experience with hands-on building helps.",
        time: "Preparation is practice rather than a long project. Teams build timed mock challenges with limited materials, work on cutting accurately, and practice dividing tasks between the two partners. Nothing is submitted ahead of time. Onsite you have 90 minutes of design and construction, and some problems also require teams to be present for testing.",
        cost: "Costs are low to moderate and mostly go into the toolbox. You need hand tools, adhesives, rulers, marking tools, a cutting surface, wax paper, and safety glasses. Practice materials add a little, but TSA supplies the actual competition materials. A school shop may lend tools, and you can build the kit gradually.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like building, tinkering, and solving surprise problems against the clock. It suits a pair of partners who can plan quickly and work well together. Students interested in engineering or design will like the hands-on challenge. Beginners are welcome if they practice with timed builds."
    },
    "future-tech-teacher": {
        materials: "You need a computer to prepare a PDF portfolio, plus whatever you will use for a short classroom activity. The portfolio includes research summaries on two educational technologies, a one page essay on three traits of a good technology or engineering teacher, a lesson plan tied to the ITEEA Standards for Technological Literacy, copies of handouts, references, a copyright checklist, and consent forms if people appear in images. In the semifinal you present the activity to judges who act as students, and you must bring any audiovisual equipment yourself, including a power strip with surge protector and an extension cord.",
        difficulty: "You are acting as a real teacher, so the work is designing a lesson that fits a specific grade level, explaining your goals, and delivering it clearly. The challenge is writing an original lesson plan with standards correlation and assessment, researching educational technologies, and teaching in a seven minute presentation without going over time. Beginners can compete, but comfort with public speaking and some teaching or tutoring experience helps. Judges interact with you as students, so you need to think on your feet.",
        time: "Preparation takes a few weeks of research, writing, and designing the lesson. Most people spend time on the research summaries, the essay, the lesson plan, and the handouts before the mid-May PDF deadline. Semifinalists then prepare and rehearse a seven minute activity presentation, with three minutes for setup and three for questions.",
        cost: "Costs are low because most of the work is research, writing, and presenting. Printing handouts and any simple teaching props are the main extras. If your activity needs materials, they can be inexpensive, and audiovisual equipment may be borrowed from your school.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who are curious about teaching and like explaining ideas. It suits individuals who enjoy planning lessons, working with younger students, and presenting. Students considering careers in education will get great practice. Beginners can join, especially if they like creating hands-on activities."
    },
    "debating-tech-issues": {
        materials: "You do not need any equipment. Instead you prepare a one sheet summary of references that covers all of the annual subtopics, and you print at least four copies to hand in. The guide allows pre-written notes on 3 by 5 inch notecards and a three-ring binder of reference material that is listed on your reference summary. Electronic devices, audio-visual materials, and cell phone timers are not allowed, so a traditional stopwatch is the only timing tool allowed.",
        difficulty: "You must be ready to argue any subtopic from either the Pro or Con side, and you only learn your side after drawing a card with five minutes to prepare. The format includes two minute speeches, cross-examination, and rebuttals, so you need fast thinking, solid research, and calm delivery. Strict civility rules apply, and aggressive behavior can lead to disqualification. Beginners can learn debate structure, but experience with speaking or debate helps a lot.",
        time: "Most of the time goes into research and practice. Teams read about each subtopic, build a pro and con case for every one, organize the reference binder, and run mock debates to practice timing and cross-examination. There is no pre-conference submission beyond the reference summary. At the conference, the prep room gives you five minutes before each debate.",
        cost: "Costs are very low. You need printing for the reference summary, notecards, a binder, and possibly a stopwatch. Research sources are usually free through the library or online.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like arguing a position, researching issues, and thinking on their feet. It suits partners who can split speaking roles and support each other in cross-examination. Students interested in law, policy, or public speaking will build strong skills. Beginners are welcome if they practice with a partner."
    },
    "extemporaneous-speech": {
        materials: "You need no equipment. At the event you draw a technology or TSA topic card, get 15 minutes to prepare, and receive up to three 3 by 5 inch blank notecards to write notes on. You cannot bring reference materials or devices into the preparation room, and your notes must be written during the preparation period. A podium is available.",
        difficulty: "You have to organize and deliver a three to five minute speech with only 15 minutes of preparation, so the challenge is thinking quickly and speaking clearly without a script. You lose points for going outside the time window by even ten seconds, and relying too heavily on notes can lower your score. Knowledge of technology and TSA helps, but delivery counts too. Beginners can improve quickly with practice, and speaking experience is a plus.",
        time: "There is no project to build or submit, so preparation is practicing speeches. Students usually draw random topics, give timed speeches, and get feedback on structure and pacing. Practicing across many technology topics helps, since you will not know the topic ahead of time. At the conference, you get 15 minutes to prepare and then deliver the speech.",
        cost: "This is one of the least expensive events because it requires no supplies. Travel to the conference is the main cost, and practice only needs a timer and willing listeners.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like public speaking, quick thinking, and talking about technology. It suits independent people who stay calm under pressure. Students who want to improve confidence in front of an audience will gain a lot. Beginners can join and grow through repeated practice."
    },
    "prepared-presentation": {
        materials: "You need a laptop that is charged and runs on battery power, because the judges view your screen on the table and projection equipment is not allowed. The presentation uses a digital slide deck with at least five slides, and the only identifying information allowed in the deck is your participant ID number. Your talk has to run between three and five minutes with no more than one minute of setup, and it must be your own work.",
        difficulty: "You research a current STEM topic from the annual theme and deliver a clear, thoughtful talk in a short window. The challenge is condensing a complex idea into a few minutes, designing clear slides, and finishing within the time limits, since going over or under costs points in ten second steps. Beginners can do well with a strong topic and practice, but delivery and slide design matter.",
        time: "Preparation takes a few weeks of research, writing the talk, and making the slides, followed by plenty of rehearsal to hit the time window. Many students practice in front of friends or teachers and revise the deck. There is no pre-conference submission, so the work is ready for the conference itself.",
        cost: "Costs are very low because a laptop and presentation software are usually all you need. Free slide tools work well. The main cost is travel to the conference.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like researching a topic and explaining it well. It suits independent speakers who enjoy designing clear slides. Students interested in communication, science, or leadership will benefit. Beginners can join and build confidence."
    },
    "forensic-science": {
        materials: "The preliminary round is a one hour test that each of the two team members takes, online or on a scan sheet. If it is a scan sheet, bring two sharpened No. 2 pencils. Semifinalists examine a mock crime scene and write an analysis, and they may bring a tool kit since TSA provides none. The guide lists items such as string, protective equipment, a 10 meter tape measure, tweezers, scissors, a flashlight, evidence bags, gloves, a clipboard, graph paper, rulers, evidence markers, a magnifying glass, and a camera (cell phones are not allowed, and photos must be deleted before leaving the holding room).",
        difficulty: "The test covers basic forensic science theory, and the semifinal requires hands-on crime scene skills, including a primary survey, processing the scene, collecting and recording trace evidence, and drawing a proportional diagram. That mix of science knowledge and careful procedure is the difficulty. You have 20 minutes to survey the scene and 20 minutes to write your analysis, so organization and teamwork are vital. Beginners can learn the basics, but study of forensic science concepts helps.",
        time: "Preparation includes studying forensic science concepts for the test and practicing mock crime scenes with a timer. Teams often assemble their tool kit and rehearse dividing tasks between the two partners. Nothing is submitted ahead of time. Onsite the test takes one hour, and semifinalists spend 20 minutes at the scene and 20 minutes writing.",
        cost: "Costs are low. The tool kit items are inexpensive, such as string, gloves, a tape measure, bags, and graph paper. Study resources are usually free, and a school science room can often lend materials.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like mysteries, lab science, and careful observation. It suits pairs who can stay organized and work together under time pressure. Students interested in criminal justice or science careers will find it a great preview. Beginners can join by studying the basics and practicing mock scenes."
    },
    "chapter-team": {
        materials: "There is nothing to build. In the preliminary round each of the six team members takes a one hour parliamentary procedure test, online or on a scan sheet, and you bring two sharpened No. 2 pencils if it is a scan sheet. For the semifinal your team wears the Chapter Team attire and the event coordinator supplies paper, pens, a calculator, notecards, a copy of the TSA creed, secretary's minutes, a treasurer's report, and a list of five parliamentary actions. You cannot bring written materials into the event room, although you may bring a timepiece.",
        difficulty: "The test covers parliamentary procedure, so you need to know motions, voting, and meeting order, and the semifinal asks you to run a complete chapter business meeting in 15 minutes with penalties for overtime. Six people have to coordinate roles as president, vice president, secretary, treasurer, reporter, and sergeant-at-arms, and the secretary must write minutes within five minutes afterward. Beginners can learn the procedures, but teams that rehearse together do much better.",
        time: "There is no pre-conference submission, so preparation is studying and rehearsing. Teams usually study parliamentary procedure for the test, then run mock meetings on a timer to practice the opening and closing ceremonies, the motions, and the secretary's minutes. At the event the test takes an hour, and teams that advance run their meeting in about 15 minutes.",
        cost: "Costs are low. The main items are the required Chapter Team attire, including a navy blazer with the official TSA patch and an official TSA tie, and study materials. Check with your advisor about whether your chapter already owns blazers and ties. The event itself requires no equipment.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like leadership, teamwork, and running organized meetings. It suits six teammates who enjoy rehearsing together and thinking quickly during a live demonstration. Students who want to become chapter or state officers will gain direct experience. Beginners can learn the procedures with practice."
    },
    "fashion-design": {
        materials: "You make a wearable prototype garment that reflects the annual theme, along with full-size, student-made patterns on lightweight vellum paper (purchased patterns are not allowed) and a documentation portfolio in a clear front report cover. The prototype, patterns, and portfolio all go together in a plastic storage container of no more than 32 quarts, and hangers and dressmaker mannequins are not allowed. Special textiles such as waterproof or fireproof fabrics are not required, though textile research belongs in the portfolio. Semifinalists need a model from their own chapter to wear the garment during the presentation.",
        difficulty: "You have to design and sew a presentation-quality garment, draft your own patterns, and explain your textile choices and construction methods. Sewing skill matters, since judges score techniques and finish, and the portfolio asks for hand-drawn sketches, computer-drawn final designs, and a literature research summary. Beginners can learn to sew a simple design, but experience with patterns and garment construction helps. The semifinal has only a short window of three minutes to present and three to answer questions.",
        time: "Expect a multi-week project. Teams research and sketch, draft patterns, test with draft fabric, sew the final prototype, and write the portfolio sections. Pattern making and construction usually take the longest. Semifinalists then prepare a short presentation and coordinate a model for the fitting.",
        cost: "Costs depend on the fabric and notions. Fabric, thread, notions such as zippers or buttons, vellum for patterns, and a plastic container are the main purchases. Practice garments add cost, and a school may provide sewing machines. Choosing affordable fabric and reusing scraps for test pieces helps control spending.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who love fashion, sewing, and creative design and who like combining those skills with technology elements from the annual theme. It suits detail-oriented teams of two to four. Students interested in apparel design or textiles will find it a great fit. Beginners can join if they are willing to learn sewing and pattern making."
    },
    "artificial-intelligence-ai": {
        materials: "There is nothing to build or submit ahead of time. The official overview describes a qualifying test on AI concepts, models, and best practices, followed by an onsite problem that semifinalists solve with a generative AI tool. What you bring, such as a laptop, and which AI tool is used are set in the current event guide and competition updates, so confirm them before you travel. Since this is one of the few TSA events where generative AI is part of the task, practice with more than one AI tool and with writing clear, specific instructions.",
        difficulty: "The test rewards solid knowledge of how AI systems work, including common model types, how they are trained, and what responsible use looks like. The semifinal is harder because your solution is measured objectively, which means you need to get a useful result from a generative AI tool within a time limit, not just try prompts and hope. Beginners can learn the concepts, but comfort with data, logic, and writing precise prompts gives a real edge. It is a team of two, so coordinating who tests and who refines matters.",
        time: "Preparation is mostly studying and practice rather than a long project. Teams usually learn the vocabulary and concepts for the test and then work through timed practice problems with an AI tool, comparing results and refining their approach. The overview does not describe a pre-conference submission, so most of the work happens in the weeks before the conference.",
        cost: "Costs stay low because most preparation uses free study resources and the AI tools your school or team can already access. Check the current guide for which tool is used at the conference so you do not pay for a subscription you do not need.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who are curious about how AI works and enjoy problem solving with technology. It suits a pair of partners who like experimenting, testing results, and explaining their reasoning. Students interested in computer science or data will get a useful head start. Beginners can join if they are willing to study the concepts first."
    },
    "automated-manufacturing-systems": {
        materials: "Teams build a sensor-based mechatronic prototype that automates part of a production process, program the automation logic, and write documentation of the mechanical and software design plus instructions that let judges operate the system. That points to a controller or microcontroller, sensors, motors or other actuators, structural materials for the frame, and a power source. The official overview does not list exact component, size, or power limits, so check the current event guide and the yearly problem before buying parts.",
        difficulty: "This event combines mechanical building, electronics, and programming in one system that must actually solve a production problem. The hard parts are making sensors and actuators work reliably together, writing control logic that handles the process correctly, and documenting both sides clearly enough that someone else can run it. Beginners can learn with a starter robotics or automation kit, but experience with electronics and code saves a lot of troubleshooting. The instructions for judges also test how well you can explain your system.",
        time: "Expect a long project. Teams generally analyze the prompt, sketch the process, build the frame, wire the sensors and actuators, write and debug the control code, and then test the whole system repeatedly. Writing the design documentation and operating instructions takes its own block of time. Leave room near the end to troubleshoot, because small wiring or logic problems are common.",
        cost: "Costs rise through the electronics and hardware. A controller, sensors, motors or actuators, wiring, power supplies, and frame materials are the main items, and spare parts add more. A school robotics or engineering lab may already have controllers and tools, and reusing parts from other projects cuts spending. Starting with a simple design and adding features only if time allows helps keep the budget in check.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like robotics, electronics, and how factories and automated systems work. It suits teams of two or three that can share building, wiring, and programming roles. Students interested in engineering or manufacturing careers will enjoy it. Beginners can join, especially with a teammate who has built circuits or robots before."
    },
    "hs-cybersecurity": {
        materials: "The official overview describes on-site Capture the Flag style challenges in which teams find and exploit weaknesses, so the preparation is skills and practice rather than a physical build. Areas named include exploit development, digital puzzles, cryptography, reverse engineering, binary analysis, and mobile security. What you may bring onsite, including a laptop, is set by the current event guide, so confirm it before you travel. Many students practice with a computer, a Linux environment, and free training platforms, and TSA's middle school cybersecurity guide lists resources such as TryHackMe and Hack The Box.",
        difficulty: "This is a hands-on technical event, so the challenge is solving real security puzzles within a limited time. You need to understand how systems fail, and different challenges draw on skills such as reading code, decoding encrypted messages, analyzing programs, and using the command line. Beginners can start with entry level capture the flag exercises and build up, but programming and networking knowledge help a lot. Teams of two to four can split categories, which makes communication important.",
        time: "Preparation is steady practice over weeks and months. Teams usually work through beginner capture the flag problems, learn the basics of cryptography and common tools, and then move to more advanced categories like reverse engineering. Reviewing solutions after each practice session is an important part of learning. There is no project to submit ahead of time in the official overview, so the time goes into skill building.",
        cost: "Costs are low because most practice platforms have free options and your team can use computers you already have. Some training platforms offer paid tiers, but they are not required to prepare. The only real requirement is access to a reliable computer you can practice on.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like puzzles, coding, and figuring out how systems can be broken and defended. It suits teams of two to four that enjoy working through problems together under a clock. Students interested in cybersecurity or computer science careers will gain valuable skills. Beginners can start with entry level challenges and grow."
    },
    "hybrid-racer-xl": {
        materials: "Teams design, build, and race a car powered by both solar and battery power. For 2026-27 the design requirement is that the car must hold or pull one 5 ounce fishing weight supplied onsite, with a hitch at the rear to attach it, and the 2027 track surface is a model solar track on top of carpet. That means you need a chassis, wheels with enough traction for carpet, a motor, a solar panel, a battery, and wiring. Exact limits on size, panels, and batteries are in the current event guide, so check them before you buy parts.",
        difficulty: "The challenge is balancing weight, traction, and power. A car that must pull or carry a fixed weight on carpet needs enough traction and torque while staying light enough for the solar and battery system to move it. Beginners can learn the engineering, but careful testing makes the difference. The event has a design process side as well, so be ready to explain why you chose your motor, gearing, and layout.",
        time: "This is a multi-week build. Teams usually plan the design around the yearly requirement, build a first car, test it on a carpet surface with a weight attached, and then adjust gearing, wheels, and wiring. Because solar performance varies with lighting, testing in different conditions takes extra sessions.",
        cost: "Costs come from the electronics and parts, including a motor, solar panel, battery, wheels, and the chassis materials. Practice cars and replacement parts add up. A school lab may provide tools and basic components, and reusing wheels or motors from earlier projects can reduce spending.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who enjoy engineering, racing, and renewable energy. It suits teams of two to four that like testing, tweaking, and improving a design. Students interested in electrical or mechanical engineering will find it a good fit. Beginners can join with a simple design and learn through testing."
    },
    "interior-design": {
        materials: "You make color samples and material design boards for the annual challenge, which for 2026-27 is designing the interior of a model tiny home with a $28,000 material budget and required spaces including a living area, kitchenette, sleeping area, bathroom, workspace, and storage. That calls for foam board or similar for the boards, printed or real samples of finishes such as flooring, paint, and fabric, and a way to draw the layout, whether by hand or with design software. Exact board sizes and submission rules are in the current event guide, so check them before you build.",
        difficulty: "The challenge is making a cohesive design that fits a real budget, a small footprint, and a clear audience. You need to choose materials and finishes that work together, account for the $28,000 budget, and explain your design choices. Beginners can learn the basics of color and layout, but a good eye for design and clear explanations make the difference. The presentation also tests how well you can interpret the design problem.",
        time: "Most of the work is research, planning, and assembling the boards. Teams usually study small space design, sketch layouts, choose finishes, and put together the design boards and any supporting materials. Revising choices to stay within budget takes time, and preparing the presentation is part of the process.",
        cost: "Costs are moderate and mostly go to board materials, printing, and samples. Foam board, adhesives, swatches or printed finishes, and print costs are the main expenses. Using free design tools and printing at school can keep costs down, and you do not need to buy the actual materials you specify.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like design, color, and space planning. It suits creative teams of two who enjoy making a design look polished and thoughtful. Students interested in architecture or interior design will find it a great introduction. Beginners can join with an interest in design and a willingness to research."
    },
    "hs-vlogging": {
        materials: "You need a camera or smartphone, a way to record clear sound, editing software, and a place to host your videos online. For 2026-27 the theme is a connected three-episode vlog series of no more than six minutes total about how one small action can set off a chain of events. Semifinalists complete an onsite challenge to produce more videos, with props, lines of dialogue, and topics revealed at the semifinalist orientation meeting, so plan to bring your own recording and editing setup to the conference. Check the current event guide for submission and equipment details.",
        difficulty: "The challenge is telling a clear story across three connected episodes in a tight time limit while keeping the video and sound quality high. Good vlogs need planning, camera presence, and efficient editing, and the semifinal adds the pressure of producing new videos from surprise requirements. Beginners can learn basic filming and editing, but practice with fast, simple workflows pays off.",
        time: "Preparation takes weeks of scripting, filming, and editing the series, and teams often reshoot scenes after reviewing their cut. Because the semifinal is a surprise challenge, practicing a quick plan-film-edit routine matters too. The exact timing for the onsite challenge is announced at the orientation meeting.",
        cost: "Costs are modest if you already have a smartphone or school camera. Free or low-cost editing programs are available, and extra gear such as a tripod, microphone, or lighting is optional. Travel to the conference is the larger expense.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like storytelling, being on camera, and editing video. It suits teams of two to six that can split writing, filming, acting, and editing. Students interested in media or social content creation will build useful skills. Beginners can start with simple stories and short clips."
    },
    "ms-audio-podcasting": {
        materials: "All you really need is a way to record voices, free or school-provided audio editing software, and a place to upload the finished file so judges can open a direct link. The podcast must run between one and five minutes, and you can add music or free, non-copyrighted sound effects as long as you describe them in your portfolio. You also submit a PDF with a timeline of every voice, a list of hardware and software, a work log, a copyright checklist, and square cover art at 1400 by 1400 pixels. Semifinalists produce a new podcast during a 36 hour window at the conference, so bring your recording and editing setup.",
        difficulty: "The challenge is telling an interesting story with only sound. You have to write a script, perform voices naturally, add sound effects, and edit so the pacing feels smooth, and timing mistakes cost points. For middle school students this is a friendly event to start with because simple recording tools are enough, but practice makes the audio sound polished. The semifinal adds surprise requirements that you must handle quickly.",
        time: "Before the conference, teams spend a few weeks writing and rehearsing the script, recording multiple takes, editing, and building the portfolio by the mid-May deadline. Editing is often the longest step because small audio mistakes are easy to hear. If you advance, you will have a short, intense production window onsite.",
        cost: "Costs are low. A smartphone or inexpensive USB microphone is enough to record, and free editing programs work well. The most useful extra is a quiet space, since background noise is hard to remove, and many schools can provide a quiet classroom.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like storytelling, acting out voices, and making sound effects. It suits creative teams, though solo entries are allowed. It is a great first media event for beginners who want to try production without expensive gear. Students who enjoy radio, podcasts, or audio editing will love it."
    },
    "ms-biotechnology": {
        materials: "Teams build a display no larger than 15 inches deep, 30 inches wide, and 48 inches high and prepare a documentation portfolio in a clear front report cover. AC electricity may not be used, so any lights or electronics must run on dry-cell batteries or photovoltaic cells, and no liquids, viruses, live plants, or animals can be part of the display. Semifinalists may bring audio-visual materials or a laptop, but projectors and screens are not allowed and power is not provided. The research can be student-performed or a recreation or simulation of work done by scientists.",
        difficulty: "You have to understand a contemporary biotechnology issue well enough to explain its opportunities, risks, and ethical questions. The display and the portfolio must tell that story clearly, and the semifinal presentation requires every team member to speak. Beginners can handle this with careful research and good sources, but explaining science simply takes practice. A minimum of three different types of references is required, so research skills matter.",
        time: "Preparation focuses on research, writing, and building. Teams typically spend weeks reading about the topic, writing the portfolio pages, and designing a display that stays within the size limit. Semifinalists then rehearse a seven minute presentation with two minutes of questions.",
        cost: "Costs are mostly the display board, printing, and a few craft supplies. A model or battery-powered feature adds a bit, while teams that keep the display simple can spend very little. A school science room or library can help with research sources.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who are curious about biology, health, or the environment and like making clear visual explanations. It suits teams of three or more that can share research, writing, building, and speaking. Students who enjoy science fair style projects will feel at home. Beginners are welcome."
    },
    "ms-career-prep": {
        materials: "You do not need equipment. You prepare a one page typed cover letter and a job-specific resume as a single PDF with no cover page, using the scenario that you have finished school and have the education and training for an entry level job in your chosen technology career. The rules ask for a fictitious home address and phone number but your real name, and they do not allow photos or headshots. Semifinalists then take part in a mock interview, so you also need appropriate TSA competition attire.",
        difficulty: "The skill is professional communication. You research a technology-related career from the annual theme, then write a persuasive cover letter and a resume that matches the job, and your qualifications must be realistic for that career. The mock interview tests how well you can answer questions about your background and the job. Beginners can do well because the format is straightforward, but polished writing and confident speaking set top entries apart.",
        time: "Most of the work is research and writing in the weeks before the mid-May PDF deadline. Students usually choose a career, study its education requirements and duties, draft and revise the cover letter and resume, and ask for feedback. Semifinalists then practice answering common interview questions for a ten minute mock interview.",
        cost: "This is one of the least expensive events. Your needs are a computer, a word processor, and printing if you want to review drafts on paper.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who are curious about careers and want practice with resumes and interviews. It suits individuals who like writing and speaking with confidence. It is a great pick for students who want skills they can use for real jobs. Beginners are welcome."
    },
    "ms-challenging-tech-issues": {
        materials: "Nothing needs to be built or submitted. You go to a preparation room 15 minutes before your presentation and may bring non-electronic reference materials, plus your own pens or pencils and optional note cards. No other help or devices are allowed in the prep room. You then give a two to four minute debate-style presentation on one of the annual topics, with your team arguing opposing views of the issue.",
        difficulty: "The difficulty is quick thinking. You have only 15 minutes to prepare and then four minutes at most to present, with no overtime allowed, so you must organize an argument and deliver it clearly under pressure. You have to understand both sides of a technology issue, since the team presents opposing views. Beginners can learn the structure of an introduction, position, and conclusion, but strong research beforehand makes the preparation time much easier.",
        time: "There is no project to submit, so your time goes into researching the annual topics and practicing. Teams usually read about each topic, write short pro and con outlines, and practice delivering them in under four minutes. At the conference the 15 minute prep window happens right before you present.",
        cost: "Costs are very low. You may want printed reference materials, note cards, and pens, and the research itself is usually free through the library or online.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who enjoy discussing big technology questions and seeing both sides of an issue. It suits partners who like to think fast and speak confidently. Students interested in debate, speech, or law will get a nice introduction. Beginners can join and learn from practice rounds."
    },
    "ms-chapter-team": {
        materials: "You need no equipment. All six team members take a one hour online parliamentary procedure test at the same time, and each member must provide a laptop because the test is online only unless officials decide otherwise. In the semifinal the event coordinator supplies minutes forms, a treasurer's report, the TSA creed, a list of parliamentary actions, paper, pens, note cards, and a gavel, and you may use a timepiece and a nonprogrammable calculator. The Chapter Team attire requirements apply in both rounds, so check the dress code section early.",
        difficulty: "You must learn parliamentary procedure well enough to answer test questions and then run a full business meeting in 15 minutes with penalties for going over. Six teammates need to coordinate as president, vice president, secretary, treasurer, reporter, and sergeant-at-arms, and the secretary must also write chapter minutes in five minutes. The guide points to Robert's Rules of Order as the standard reference, and beginners can learn it with practice.",
        time: "There is no pre-conference submission. Preparation is studying and rehearsing, and teams usually practice the opening, items of business, motions, and closing many times with a timer. Studying for the test can happen individually, and then the whole team needs to rehearse together. Onsite the test is one hour and the semifinal meeting is about 15 minutes.",
        cost: "Costs are low. The main items are the required Chapter Team attire and study materials, and the event needs no equipment. Ask your advisor about borrowing or sharing blazers and ties if your chapter has them.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like leadership, teamwork, and running meetings. It suits six teammates who want to practice speaking and thinking quickly together. Students who may someday serve as chapter officers will gain direct experience. Beginners can learn the procedures with consistent practice."
    },
    "ms-childrens-stories": {
        materials: "You create an original illustrated storybook that closes to no more than 12 by 12 inches, with a story of up to 1,500 words and at least seven original illustrations. Illustrations must be freehand or computer-generated from basic lines and shapes by your team, with no templates or copyrighted material, and the book must be bound by your team rather than professionally. You also prepare a portfolio with photos of the construction process, a work log, and summaries of at least two field tests in which you read the book to children in your target age range. Drop off the book and portfolio together at the conference.",
        difficulty: "The event asks you to write, illustrate, build, and test one finished product. The hardest parts are drawing original art for every page, keeping the story within the word limit and under ten minutes to read, and arranging field tests with real young readers. Beginners can compete with a simple, well-told story, but neatness and craftsmanship matter. Semifinalists spend five minutes answering judges' questions about their book.",
        time: "This is a multi-week project. Teams usually write and edit the story first, then sketch and finish the illustrations, build the book, and schedule at least two readings with children. Documenting construction with photos and writing the research and project summaries takes extra time, and everything has to be ready to submit at the conference.",
        cost: "Costs are low to moderate and go mostly to art and book-making supplies. Paper or cardstock, drawing or painting materials, and binding supplies are the main purchases, and pop-up or interactive features need extra paper and glue. Printing the portfolio is a small additional cost.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who enjoy writing, drawing, and making crafts, and who like working with younger kids. It suits creative individuals or teams who want to turn a story idea into a physical book. Reading to children as part of the field tests is a highlight for many students. Beginners can do well with a short story and neat work."
    },
    "ms-coding": {
        materials: "All you need is a laptop and the programming tools you plan to use. The preliminary test is online, so each of the two team members brings a laptop (paper tests are only used if officials decide they are needed). For the two hour semifinal, each person brings a charged laptop, an optional mouse, pens, paper, and an optional power bank. There is no electricity or internet, and hotspots are not permitted, so every software tool you need has to be downloaded and working before you arrive. Generative AI tools are not allowed.",
        difficulty: "The test covers coding concepts such as language syntax, data structures, control flow, and object oriented programming, and the semifinal asks you to solve a set of coding problems with objectively correct answers in two hours. You must write working code without internet help or AI assistance and then demonstrate it to judges. Beginners can compete if they have learned a language like Python or Java, but regular practice solving problems is what builds speed.",
        time: "There is no pre-conference project, so preparation is practice. Most students study the concepts on the test and then solve practice problems against a timer, building familiarity with their chosen language. Make sure your offline development setup is ready, since the semifinal gives no internet access. Onsite, the test takes an hour and the semifinal challenge two hours.",
        cost: "Costs are very low because free programming languages and editors are widely available, and you only need a laptop. A power bank is optional. The main cost to plan for is travel to the conference.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who enjoy programming puzzles and want to sharpen their problem-solving. It suits partners who like to code and test quickly under a clock. Students aiming for computer science will find it a great skills builder. Beginners can start with a simple language and grow from there."
    },
    "ms-community-service-video": {
        materials: "You need a camera or smartphone, editing software, and a place to host the video online so judges can open a direct link. The video can be no longer than two minutes, all footage and graphics must be created by your team, and the year must appear at the start. Your PDF portfolio includes a one page project overview, references, a copyright checklist, consent forms for anyone shown on camera, and a work log. Semifinalists present for seven minutes and answer questions for three.",
        difficulty: "The challenge is telling the story of your chapter's real community service project in just two minutes. You need to show what your chapter did, why it mattered, and who benefited, with clear shots and sound. Beginners can do well because the story is real and personal, but planning shots ahead of time makes editing much smoother. The semifinal also asks you to explain the project in a short presentation.",
        time: "The video needs a real service project first, so the timeline depends on when your chapter does the project. After that, most teams spend a few weeks planning, filming, editing, and writing the portfolio before the mid-May deadline. Collecting signed consent forms for people on camera should happen while filming. Semifinalists then prepare a seven minute presentation.",
        cost: "Costs are low. A smartphone or school camera and free editing software are enough, and the service project itself often costs little. Any supplies for the project are separate from the event, and your chapter may already budget for them.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like volunteering and storytelling. It suits teams or solo entrants who enjoy filming and editing a short video. Students who want to highlight their chapter's impact will find it rewarding. Beginners are welcome."
    },
    "ms-cad-foundations": {
        materials: "You bring your own computer hardware, with one computer and one monitor per student, a USB port, the CAD software you will use (downloaded software is preferred since TSA does not provide internet access), and a blank USB drive labeled with your student ID for submission. Reference materials must be printed, because online resources are not allowed, and you also bring pencils. A power strip and a 20 foot extension cord are optional and not provided by TSA. TSA provides sketching paper and electricity.",
        difficulty: "You create a 2D graphic representation of an engineering part, possibly from an isometric drawing, in two hours. The skill is using your CAD software accurately and quickly and saving the drawing as a PDF without help. Beginners can learn 2D CAD, but timed practice makes a big difference. If there is a tie, completion time breaks it, so speed counts as well.",
        time: "There is no pre-conference submission. Preparation is practice, including working through sample drawings on a timer and learning to export a PDF quickly. Onsite, expect 30 minutes of setup, two hours to draw, and about an hour for the final evaluation process. Saving your work every fifteen minutes is advised.",
        cost: "Costs are low if you already have a laptop that runs your CAD software. The extras are a USB drive, pencils, and printed references. Many CAD programs have free education licenses, and school labs can help with practice time.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like drawing, drafting, and precise computer work. It suits individuals who enjoy learning a software tool well and solving a design problem quickly. Students thinking about engineering or architecture will get a helpful head start. Beginners can compete with steady practice."
    },
    "ms-construction-challenge": {
        materials: "You build a student-made scale model or prototype of a construction project that answers the annual theme, set on a display no larger than 15 inches deep, 30 inches wide, and 48 inches high. AC electricity may not be used, so any lights or moving parts must run on dry-cell batteries or photovoltaic cells that fit inside the display. Your portfolio in a clear front report cover includes a design brief, sketches, a materials list, a short explanation of why you chose major materials, drawings, and a description of any regulations or design standards that shaped the project. Model materials are your team's choice.",
        difficulty: "The challenge is planning a construction project and showing it in a believable scale model. You have to think about materials, drawings, and building standards, and then explain your choices in a seven minute presentation with three minutes of questions. Beginners can do well with careful planning and neat modeling, but understanding how real structures are designed makes your explanation stronger.",
        time: "Expect a few weeks. Teams typically sketch ideas, choose materials, draw the plans, build the scale model, and write the design brief and materials list. The guide asks chapters to take part in most stages of the project, so work is shared across the team. Semifinalists then rehearse the presentation and interview.",
        cost: "Costs depend on the model. Foam board, balsa, cardboard, craft supplies, and a display board are the main purchases, and added lights or moving parts need batteries or small solar cells. Printing the portfolio adds a smaller amount. Using scrap materials and simple finishes keeps spending down.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like building models, drawing plans, and thinking about how structures solve real problems. It suits teams of two or more who can split design, drawing, building, and speaking. Students interested in architecture or civil engineering will find it a fun start. Beginners are welcome."
    },
    "ms-data-science": {
        materials: "Teams use the data set that TSA posts with the annual topic, so there is no need to collect your own data. You need a way to analyze it and make statistical graphs, such as a spreadsheet or another tool of your choice, and a display no larger than 15 inches deep, 30 inches wide, and 48 inches high. AC power is not allowed, and any digital display must run on batteries or solar cells and fit within the display space. Your portfolio in a clear front cover includes a summary of the issue, your data analysis, findings, supporting materials, and at least three different types of references.",
        difficulty: "The challenge is turning a given data set into clear graphs and an honest conclusion about what it shows. You have to find patterns, show causes, and discuss the social and ethical impact in a five minute presentation. Beginners can learn to make charts in a spreadsheet, but reading the data carefully and explaining what the graphs mean takes practice. No outside help is allowed during the interview.",
        time: "Most of the time goes into learning the data set, making charts, and writing the portfolio over a few weeks. Teams also design the display so the graphs are the focus. Semifinalists then rehearse a five minute presentation and five minutes of questions.",
        cost: "Costs are low. The data is free, and spreadsheet programs do the analysis. You pay mostly for the display board and printing the portfolio and graphs.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like math, graphs, and finding stories in data. It suits teams of two or three who enjoy research and design. Students interested in statistics, science, or business will gain a useful skill. Beginners can start with a spreadsheet."
    },
    "ms-digital-photography": {
        materials: "For the preliminary round you submit a PDF portfolio of unified photographs, each shown with its original and edited version and followed by a description page that lists camera details such as f-stop, exposure time, ISO, and focal length. You need a camera and editing software, and cell phone cameras are permitted for the semifinal. Semifinalists bring a charged laptop, a tripod, a camera that can mount on one, a way to transfer images such as a media reader, and any optional filters or reflectors. There is no power or internet onsite.",
        difficulty: "You have to take photos around a theme, edit them in an honest and tasteful way, and explain your choices. The semifinal gives you two hours to complete a new challenge and then just three minutes to present and answer questions. Beginners can compete because a phone camera is allowed, but understanding composition and exposure improves results quickly.",
        time: "Preliminary work is a few weeks of planning shots, photographing, editing, and writing descriptions before the mid-May PDF deadline. The semifinal has a half hour for setup, two hours for the challenge, and a very short presentation, so practicing a quick shoot-and-edit routine helps.",
        cost: "Costs are low because a phone camera is allowed. A tripod and editing software are the main extras, and many editing programs are free. Printing is not needed because the portfolio is a PDF.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like taking photos and learning how to make them better. It suits independent, creative people who enjoy editing. It is a great first media event for beginners because phone cameras are allowed. Students interested in art or media will enjoy it."
    },
    "ms-dragster": {
        materials: "You build a race-worthy CO2 dragster to the published specifications, using only the materials the rules allow, and submit a two-view metric drawing on paper no larger than 11 by 17 inches with a letter-size materials list printed on or attached to the back. The drawing can be hand drafted or done in CAD, and the car is shaped with tools your school allows. TSA provides the CO2 cartridges for the race. A car that misses any specification or tolerance is disqualified, so a set of calipers or a precise ruler is worth having for checking your work.",
        difficulty: "The event rewards accuracy. The car must meet exact dimensions, and then it races 20 meters in a double-elimination bracket, so a small mistake in measuring or drilling can end your run. You also draw the car to engineering standards and answer questions in a five minute interview if you reach the semifinal. Beginners can succeed by following the specifications carefully, and a shop teacher can help with the tools.",
        time: "Expect several weeks. Most students sketch ideas and draw the car, shape the body, drill the holes carefully, sand and finish it, and recheck every measurement. Many builders make a spare body because mistakes in cutting cannot be undone. No repairs are allowed once the car is submitted, so final checks matter.",
        cost: "Costs include blocks of wood or foam, wheels, axles and hardware, and finishing supplies such as sandpaper and paint. A school shop typically provides the cutting and drilling tools, which keeps your own spending down. Extra bodies for practice are the main way costs grow, so measure carefully before you cut.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who enjoy shaping a car by hand, thinking about speed, and drafting precise drawings. It suits patient individuals who like details and racing. Students interested in engineering or design will like the combination. Beginners can join with careful measuring."
    },
    "ms-drone-challenge-uav": {
        materials: "Your drone can be bought commercially or built by your team, and it can be controlled by a gamepad or a smartphone or tablet, with RC or Wi-Fi control. With everything attached it must fit within 9 inches wide, 9 inches long, and 5 inches high, and weigh no more than 3.5 ounces (100 grams), with optional propeller guards that stay inside those limits. FPV goggles are not allowed, and if you use Wi-Fi you must rename the drone to your TSA team ID. Bring spare propellers and batteries, safety glasses, and safety vests, and prepare a documentation portfolio that explains the problem and up to three ways drones can be used for the annual theme.",
        difficulty: "The event is about safe, accurate manual flying. Each attempt lasts one minute with a timer that never pauses, and your team chooses which missions to try for points, so you need good piloting and quick decisions. Every team member must pilot at least one of up to three attempts, and the best two are added together. Safety rules are strict, and you also must understand the principles of flight for the portfolio and interview. Beginners can learn to fly small drones with practice.",
        time: "Preparation is mostly flight practice and writing the portfolio. Teams spend weeks flying their drone in a space like a gym, practicing mission-style maneuvers within a one minute window, and rotating pilots and spotters. The portfolio takes a smaller amount of time. Onsite you have time to set up, a 30 minute test session with a safety check, and a ten minute interview for semifinalists.",
        cost: "Costs depend on whether you buy or build. A small ready-to-fly drone is a straightforward purchase, while a custom build costs more. Spare propellers, batteries, a controller or tablet, and safety vests add to the total. A school may be able to lend a drone, and keeping to a simple drone under the weight limit controls spending.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who enjoy flying drones, practicing precise maneuvers, and learning how flight works. It suits a team of two that can alternate between pilot and spotter. Students interested in aviation or technology will enjoy it. Beginners can join, and many practice sessions pay off quickly."
    },
    "ms-electrical-applications": {
        materials: "The preliminary test is online, so each of the two team members brings a laptop. For the semifinal you bring your own toolkit with your school name, address, and advisor cell number on it, and the guide's suggested list includes a solderless breadboard, capacitors in several values, an S106B1 SCR, wire strippers, a digital multimeter, and a standard four-function calculator (scientific calculators are not allowed). TSA supplies paper and any other equipment needed for the onsite problem. You assemble a circuit from a schematic using your own parts and then make electrical measurements.",
        difficulty: "The event has two parts, and both need real understanding. The test covers basic electrical and electronic theory, and then you have one hour to build a circuit from a schematic and take correct measurements before answering interview questions about it. Reading schematics, placing parts on a breadboard, and using a multimeter are the practical skills. Beginners can learn them in a few sessions with a kit, but hands-on practice is the best preparation.",
        time: "Preparation is studying and building practice circuits. Teams usually learn the theory for the test, build circuits from sample schematics on a timer, and practice using a multimeter. Nothing needs to be submitted before the conference. Onsite there is a one hour test, and semifinalists get one hour to build and measure and then an interview.",
        cost: "Costs are low to moderate. The main purchase is the electronics kit, which includes a breadboard, components, wire strippers, and a multimeter, and school labs can often lend items. A four-function calculator and printed study materials are small extras.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like electronics, circuits, and hands-on tinkering. It suits a pair of partners who enjoy building from diagrams and checking results with a meter. Students interested in electrical engineering will love it. Beginners can join with a starter kit."
    },
    "ms-flight": {
        materials: "You build a glider designed to launch from a catapult. For the preliminary round you bring a pre-built glider and test it for four flights with no extra trim time, using catapults that TSA supplies. Your portfolio includes a full-scale metric technical drawing on 11 by 17 inch paper (CAD or mechanical drawing, not a freehand sketch), photos of two test gliders with flight logs, an illustration of compliance with the rules, and a technical review of one flight log. For the semifinal you rebuild a glider in 45 minutes from blanks you bring, using a toolbox up to 20 by 10 by 10 inches, cyanoacrylate glue, clay for ballast, and safety eyewear, since the guide requires CA glue and safety eyewear.",
        difficulty: "Making a glider fly well takes patience and aerodynamic sense, since small changes to wing shape or balance affect flight time. The semifinal adds time pressure because you must cut and assemble a new glider from blanks in 45 minutes and then trim it in ten, with CA glue that you should practice with beforehand. The scored technical drawing must show every part at full scale. Beginners can learn from simple designs, but building speed and neat joints come from repetition.",
        time: "Preparation is a cycle of building test gliders, launching them, and logging each flight. Teams spend weeks refining a design, making the technical drawing, and writing the portfolio items. Because the semifinal requires rebuilding in 45 minutes, many students also practice timed builds. Onsite, the preliminary flights happen first and semifinalists then reconstruct.",
        cost: "Costs are low to moderate. Balsa or similar material for several test gliders, CA glue and accelerant, clay, basic hand tools, and safety eyewear are the main purchases, and a home catapult is an inexpensive build. Printing the full-size drawing adds a little. Making extra gliders is the main way costs rise.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like building light aircraft and testing them over and over to improve. It suits two partners who enjoy careful hand work and aerodynamics. Students interested in aviation will enjoy it. Beginners can start with a simple glider and practice."
    },
    "ms-forensic-technology": {
        materials: "The preliminary test is online, so each of the two team members brings a laptop. In the semifinal you demonstrate one forensic skill to the judges, and TSA posts three skills each year under Themes and Problems that you must all be ready to show. Your team brings all of its own supplies because TSA provides none, so the materials depend on the skill you are assigned. You learn which skill you will demonstrate only after the test.",
        difficulty: "The test covers basic forensic science theory, and then you have to demonstrate one of three posted techniques in seven minutes and answer questions for three more. The challenge is being ready for any of the three, since you find out which one only after the test. You also need to be tidy, because the demonstration area must be cleaned in three minutes. Beginners can learn the techniques with practice and a good guide.",
        time: "Preparation is studying forensic basics and practicing all three posted skills until each demonstration fits in seven minutes. Teams usually assemble supply kits for every skill ahead of time. There is no project to turn in beforehand. Onsite there is a one hour test, then a short setup, demonstration, and cleanup if you advance.",
        cost: "Costs are low but depend on the posted skills. You will buy or borrow supplies for each demonstration, such as basic lab or evidence materials, and then keep them organized. A school science classroom can often help with supplies.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like science, mysteries, and showing how a technique works. It suits a pair who can explain and demonstrate smoothly. Students interested in forensic science or criminal justice will find it a fun first step. Beginners are welcome."
    },
    "ms-inventions": {
        materials: "The event is built around recycled materials. Your team designs a scaled model or prototype of a device, system, or process made mostly from recycled items, and it can be a conceptual or a working model. It sits on a static display no larger than 15 inches deep, 30 inches wide, and 48 inches high that explains the need, your brainstorming, how recycled items were used, and how you built it, styled like a product showcase for venture capitalists. AC power is not allowed, so any electronics must run on dry-cell batteries or solar cells, and liquids, live plants, and animals are not permitted.",
        difficulty: "The challenge is spotting a real need and then inventing something realistic that can be made from recycled materials. Judges look at how well the invention solves the problem, the quality of the model, and a seven minute sales pitch where they act as investors, so you must be creative and persuasive. Beginners can compete because the prototype can be conceptual, but a clear need and a neat model stand out.",
        time: "Teams usually spend a few weeks brainstorming needs, picking one, sketching ideas, collecting recycled items, and building and refining the model. The display and pitch take extra time to prepare. Semifinalists then rehearse a one minute setup, a seven minute pitch, and two minutes of questions.",
        cost: "Costs stay low because the invention is supposed to use mostly recycled materials. You may still buy glue, paint, a display board, and small parts, and any batteries or solar cells for working features. Collecting items from home or school keeps expenses down.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who love inventing, recycling, and coming up with practical solutions. It suits teams of two to four who enjoy brainstorming, building, and pitching. Students interested in entrepreneurship or engineering will enjoy the investor-style presentation. Beginners are welcome."
    },
    "ms-leadership-strategies": {
        materials: "You do not need equipment. Each of the three team members brings a pen to the preparation room, and no reference materials or devices are allowed there. The team gets 15 minutes to prepare and up to three 3 by 5 inch blank notecards, and any notes must be written during that preparation period. The presentation then runs three to five minutes, and every team member must take part.",
        difficulty: "The skill is solving a realistic chapter-officer problem quickly. Sample topics in the guide include school rules that limit travel, fundraising policies, a chapter whose members are not engaged, and a lack of community support, and you must propose a clear plan in the time allowed. Time limits are strict, with a point lost for every ten seconds over or under. Beginners can do well with an outline approach and practice.",
        time: "There is no pre-conference submission, so preparation is practicing. Teams usually run sample scenarios, organize ideas into short outlines, and practice speaking in turns within three to five minutes. The 15 minute preparation window at the conference is the real test of that practice.",
        cost: "Costs are very low because you only need pens and travel to the conference. Practice sessions can be done with your chapter at no cost.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who want to become chapter leaders and enjoy solving problems as a team. It suits groups of three that can quickly agree on a plan and share the speaking. Students who like debate or student government will feel at home. Beginners can join and learn by practicing scenarios."
    },
    "ms-mass-production": {
        materials: "Your team creates a marketable product that fits the annual theme and makes three identical copies of it on a production line, but only one full-size prototype is submitted onsite. The prototype must be displayed in a space no larger than 15 inches deep, 24 inches wide, and 24 inches high. Your portfolio in a clear front report cover includes a product description, sketches, an orthographic drawing with three views, an assembly drawing, a materials list with sizes and market value, a list of tools and machines, a production flow chart, and photos proving that at least three identical copies were made. Materials and tools are your team's choice, and you must say which parts are student produced and which are standard or purchased.",
        difficulty: "Mass Production is about planning a manufacturing process, not just making one good product. You have to design a product that can be repeated three times, draw it for production, set up tooling, run a trial, and improve the process, then explain it in a seven minute presentation. Beginners can learn the process with a simple product, but craftsmanship, consistency, and clear documentation make the biggest difference.",
        time: "Expect a multi-week project. Teams usually research ideas, make a prototype, draw it and plan production, build jigs or tooling, run a trial, and then produce several copies while photographing the process. Writing the portfolio takes its own stretch of time. Semifinalists then prepare the presentation and interview.",
        cost: "Costs come from making three or more copies of the product, so the price of materials is multiplied. A simple product made from inexpensive materials keeps spending low, while more complex materials or purchased subassemblies raise it. School shop tools and machines can often be used at no cost.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like making things, planning production, and improving a process. It suits teams of three or more that can organize roles on a mini assembly line. Students interested in manufacturing or industrial design will like it. Beginners can start with a simple product."
    },
    "ms-mechanical-engineering": {
        materials: "Teams build a catapult that shoots cornhole bags, and the rules are specific. It must be made entirely of PVC pipe, except for the launch mechanism, firing mechanism, fasteners, and safety items, which may be wood or metal. The catapult cannot have wheels or weigh more than 15 pounds, it must fit within 24 inches tall, 24 inches long, and 18 inches wide when loaded, and it needs a pull cord of at least five feet. TSA provides two 25 pound plate weights for ballast, and you bring safety goggles and a tape measure of at least 25 feet. Your portfolio covers the engineering design process, a bill of materials, sketches, and references.",
        difficulty: "The goal is consistent accuracy. You have to build a catapult that is light, stays within the footprint, and still launches bags reliably at a target, using a spring or similar mechanism of your choice. The rules limit your materials, so clever design matters, and the semifinal brackets are head-to-head one minute shootouts. Beginners can learn a lot because the build is mostly PVC, but testing and tuning take patience. The portfolio requires an explanation of the engineering design process you used.",
        time: "Teams usually spend weeks designing the mechanism, building a first version, and then testing and adjusting over many sessions. Practice shooting at a cornhole board helps you tune distance and accuracy. The portfolio and drawings are written alongside the build. At the conference you get three minutes of practice and two minutes to score eight bags, then top teams enter a bracket.",
        cost: "Costs are moderate. PVC pipe and fittings, springs or elastic, fasteners, and a few wood or metal parts for the launch and firing mechanisms are the main purchases, and a pull cord and safety goggles are small extras. Practice requires cornhole bags and a board. A school shop can often supply tools, and building a lighter, simpler design saves money.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like building, testing, and tuning a machine for accuracy. It suits a pair of partners who enjoy hands-on engineering and a little friendly competition. Students interested in mechanical engineering will enjoy applying design principles. Beginners can start with a simple lever and spring design."
    },
    "ms-medical-technology": {
        materials: "Your team makes three connected pieces. The first is a stand-alone display no larger than 15 inches deep, 30 inches wide, and 48 inches high covering the disease or condition, its science, and its social impact. The second is a folded pamphlet made from a single sheet no bigger than 8.5 by 11 inches with a QR code to your video. The third is a two to four minute multimedia video, hosted so judges can watch it without a password or download. AC power is not allowed, so any electronics must use dry-cell batteries or solar cells.",
        difficulty: "The challenge is explaining a medical technology clearly to a patient or family, covering how it works, its risks, costs, and what to expect. You must understand the science and then communicate it simply in a display, a pamphlet, and a short video. The semifinal is a short interview with only four minutes of questions, so you need to know your topic well. Beginners can do well with credible sources and careful writing.",
        time: "Most of the time goes into research, then writing and designing the display and pamphlet and filming and editing the video. The video and QR code need to be tested so judges can access them easily. Teams usually spend a few weeks, then rehearse for the short interview.",
        cost: "Costs are moderate and mostly cover the display board, printing the pamphlet and sources, and any model or props. The video can be made with a phone and free editing software, but good lighting or a microphone is helpful. A model is not required.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who are interested in medicine, health, and explaining science to others. It suits teams of two or three who like research, design, and video. Students thinking about healthcare careers will find it motivating. Beginners are welcome."
    },
    "ms-microcontroller-design": {
        materials: "You design a working digital device that is controlled by a programmed microcontroller, and it can be no larger than 18 by 12 by 12 inches. The device can use AC power or a dry cell battery, and remote control technology is allowed. Your portfolio includes your source code, a materials list, a team evaluation, a work log, and references. You also need a short presentation, since you have three minutes to explain the problem and demonstrate the device. The guide's resource list points to microcontroller tutorials for getting started.",
        difficulty: "You have to combine electronics, programming, and design in one product that solves a real problem. The microcontroller needs to be wired correctly, the code must work reliably, and the final device should look well made. Beginners can start with a popular beginner microcontroller board and simple sensors, but debugging circuits and code takes patience. The three minute presentation requires a clear explanation of your device.",
        time: "This is a project spanning several weeks. Teams usually choose a problem, prototype the circuit, write and test the code, build the enclosure, and then document the process. The portfolio asks for source code and a materials list, so keep notes as you build. The final presentation is short, so rehearse it.",
        cost: "Costs depend on your device. A microcontroller board, sensors, a power source, wiring, and an enclosure are the main purchases, and school labs often have kits. Reusing parts from other projects keeps costs down, and a simpler device is easier to complete and test.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like electronics and programming and want to make a device that works. It suits individuals or teams who enjoy prototyping and debugging. Students interested in computer or electrical engineering will love it. Beginners can start with a simple project."
    },
    "ms-off-the-grid": {
        materials: "You build a display and a model for a sustainable home design in a specified country, and the model can show either the whole home or the sustainable system you highlight. The display and model together fit within 15 inches deep, 30 inches wide, and 48 inches high, with the portfolio kept inside, and must include a world map marking the location. AC power is not allowed, so any electronics use dry-cell batteries or solar cells. The portfolio includes CAD drawings on 11 by 17 inch sheets, a bill of materials that stays within the budget in the design brief, a description of the country's climate and resources, alternative solutions, and at least three credible references.",
        difficulty: "The challenge is designing a believable off-the-grid solution that fits a particular climate, infrastructure, and budget. That means researching a real place, choosing sustainable systems such as energy or water solutions, and keeping the bill of materials within the budget. The CAD drawings add a technical drawing skill, and the semifinal asks you to present and defend your design. Beginners can succeed with strong research, but CAD drawing requires some learning.",
        time: "Most teams spend several weeks on research, design, drawing, model building, and writing. Learning enough CAD to produce the schematics and diagrams can take the longest. The portfolio and budget have to match the model. Semifinalists prepare a seven minute presentation and three minutes of questions.",
        cost: "Costs are moderate and come mostly from the model and display. Foam board, craft supplies, and small parts for the model, plus printing for the large drawings, are the main expenses. Any lights or moving parts need batteries or solar cells. CAD software can often be free through a school.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who care about sustainability and enjoy design, research, and model building. It suits teams who like to learn about other countries and explain a solution to judges. Students interested in architecture or environmental engineering will love it. Individuals can enter too, and beginners are welcome."
    },
    "ms-prepared-speech": {
        materials: "You need no equipment. You deliver a three to five minute speech on the theme of the current National TSA Conference, and you may use note cards. Props, computers, display boards, and costumes are not allowed, and you cannot reveal your school, chapter, or city. TSA provides a podium. Your speech must be your own work.",
        difficulty: "The challenge is giving a memorable speech without any visual aids to lean on. You must stay between three and five minutes, since a point is lost for every ten seconds over or under, and keep the audience engaged with only your voice and presence. Judges score both the content and your stage presence. Beginners can improve quickly with rehearsal, and speaking experience gives a clear advantage.",
        time: "There is no project to submit, so preparation is writing and rehearsing. Students usually draft the speech, cut and reshape it, and practice many times with a timer in front of friends or a coach. Practicing in front of people helps with nerves. At the conference you give the speech in your heat and, if you advance, in the semifinal.",
        cost: "This is one of the least expensive events. You need note cards and possibly a printed copy of the speech to practice, and the main cost is travel to the conference.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like public speaking and want to improve their confidence. It suits individuals who enjoy writing and performing a speech. Students interested in debate, theater, or leadership will like it. Beginners can start with a short, clear message."
    },
    "ms-problem-solving": {
        materials: "TSA provides the problem and materials onsite, and you may use only what is issued, except for glue and masking tape from your toolbox. You must bring a toolbox no larger than 20 by 10 by 10 inches labeled with your school name, address, and advisor cell number, and teams without one cannot compete. The guide's suggested list includes non-electric cutting tools, adhesives (no aerosol or electric applicators), a cutting surface (required), rulers, marking tools, pliers or wrenches as needed, wax paper, and one roll of masking tape no wider than 3/4 inch. Safety eyewear is required, and another team verifies your toolbox contents.",
        difficulty: "You receive an unseen problem and have 90 minutes to design and build a solution that is judged by a finite measure such as time, distance, height, or strength. The challenge is to plan quickly, build carefully with limited materials, and work well with your partner. Because the problem is a surprise, general building skills and practice with similar challenges matter most. Beginners can learn from practice problems.",
        time: "There is no project submission, so preparation is practice. Teams often build timed practice challenges with limited materials, practice measuring and cutting accurately, and decide how to split tasks. Onsite you get 90 minutes to design and construct, and your solution is tested afterward.",
        cost: "Costs are low and are mostly your toolbox. You bring hand tools, adhesives, rulers, wax paper, tape, and safety glasses, and TSA provides the actual building materials. A school shop may lend tools, and practice materials can be inexpensive.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like hands-on challenges and thinking creatively under pressure. It suits a pair of partners who communicate well and enjoy building. Students interested in engineering will enjoy the quick design and test cycle. Beginners are welcome."
    },
    "ms-promotional-marketing": {
        materials: "You need a computer with design software to create a three-part marketing portfolio. The parts are a printable advertisement, a wearable design that is viewable as a PDF, and digital signage made in presentation software and submitted as a link. Your portfolio PDF also includes notes on what is original and what is cited, the software you used, a copyright checklist, and consent forms if people appear in images. Semifinalists bring a charged laptop, pens, paper, and an optional mouse and power bank for a one hour onsite design challenge, with no templates allowed and clip art only from your own graphic library.",
        difficulty: "This event tests design range. You have to produce a printed ad, a wearable design, and scrolling digital signage that all respond to the same theme and required elements. Each piece needs good layout, color, and clear messaging, and the semifinal gives only one hour to design something new on a laptop. Beginners can learn the tools, but practice with layout and presentation software is the best preparation.",
        time: "Preliminary work takes a few weeks of planning a visual identity, designing all three pieces, documenting sources and software, and testing the digital signage link before the mid-May deadline. Semifinalists then need a quick 15 minute setup and one hour for the challenge, so timed practice helps.",
        cost: "Costs are low because everything is digital. Free design tools can create the ad and signage, and the wearable design is submitted as a PDF rather than manufactured. The main requirement is a laptop with design software, which many schools provide.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like graphic design, advertising, and promoting an idea. It suits independent creative people who enjoy working with layout, color, and short messages. Students interested in marketing or media will gain practical skills. Beginners can try it with free design tools."
    },
    "ms-robotics": {
        materials: "You build an open-source robot, starting from a kit or from separate parts, with a structure of plastic, metal, wood, or 3D-printed material. Batteries must be commercially available lithium-ion or lithium polymer packs, and the robot must fit in a 16 inch cube at the start and have removable covers for inspection. Pit gear includes a radio controller, chargers, a toolbox, spare parts, a power strip, an extension cord, and your computer and software, plus two welding blankets of at least 4 by 6 feet if you bring any powered tool or soldering iron. Safety glasses and safety vests are required, and drones are not permitted in this event.",
        difficulty: "The challenge is building a robot that can navigate a level course and complete the yearly task within a ten minute clock. You must choose a drive method that fits the surface, keep the electronics dependable, and drive with control. Strict battery and pit safety rules add pressure. Beginners can start from a kit, but the team needs time to practice driving and fixing problems.",
        time: "Robotics is a project of several weeks to months. Teams plan a design around the yearly challenge, build and wire the robot, code or tune controls if needed, and spend many sessions driving the course and making repairs. At the conference there are 10 minutes to set up, a 30 minute test and safety session, the ten minute run, and an interview for semifinalists.",
        cost: "Costs add up through the kit or parts, motors, controller, batteries, charger, and spare parts. Safety vests and welding blankets add smaller amounts. A school robotics program may lend tools or a kit, and starting with a simple design helps keep costs manageable.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who love building and driving robots and enjoy teamwork. It suits teams of two to six who can share building, wiring, coding, and driving. Students interested in engineering will enjoy it. Beginners can join with a kit and a mentor."
    },
    "ms-solar-racer": {
        materials: "Teams build a solar-powered model car that stays under 60 cm long, 30 cm wide, and 30 cm high, with one solar panel of no more than 3.2 watts and the motor from the supplied kit. Materials for the car must cost less than $50, not counting the panel, with receipts recorded and recycled materials documented. The car needs a mounted holder for two AA batteries with a switch and button for the starting line, a student-designed attachment that rides on a guide wire, and a visible 3 cm square for your team ID. You also build a showcase display no larger than 15 by 30 by 48 inches with design drawings, a run log of at least five runs, and a QR code linking to three videos of the car traveling 20 meters on solar power alone.",
        difficulty: "The challenge is making a light, efficient car that runs straight on a guide wire, since the conference races are indoors on provided batteries with the panel still attached. Small choices in weight, wheels, and friction change speed a lot, and rules prohibit changing the panel or motor. You also document each step of the engineering design process on a display and answer interview questions. Beginners can succeed by testing carefully and following the rules.",
        time: "Expect several weeks of designing, building, and testing. Teams need many test runs on a 20 meter track to fill in the run log, and the three solar-only videos require sunny conditions or a strong light source. The display and drawings are completed alongside the build. At the event, cars make time trials and top cars race in a head-to-head bracket.",
        cost: "Costs are capped by the rules, since the car's materials cannot exceed $50 not including the panel, and the recycled materials do not count toward that limit. You pay for the kit components, wheels, axles, and body materials, and the display board and printing add a bit. Reusing items and keeping the design simple keeps spending low.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like building, testing, and optimizing a small vehicle. It suits teams of two to four who enjoy engineering and renewable energy. Students interested in science or engineering will like the combination of building and documenting. Beginners are welcome."
    },
    "ms-stem-animation": {
        materials: "You need a computer with animation or video editing software of your choice and a place to host the finished animation online with a direct link. Your animation can run no longer than three minutes, and all graphics, backgrounds, and audio must be originally created by your team. Purchased content such as textures, models, and royalty-free music is not allowed, and neither are purchased items from sites like Powtoon or Animaker. Your PDF portfolio includes images of a hand-sketched storyboard (no digital screen captures), a written description, a software and hardware list, references, a work log, and a copyright checklist. The guide's resource list points to free beginner tools such as Khan Academy's Pixar in a Box and AnimateSpace.",
        difficulty: "The challenge is making a clear, accurate animation that teaches a STEM idea in three minutes or less, using only art and sound you create. You have to plan with a storyboard, animate smoothly, and keep everything original, which takes patience. Beginners can use free tutorials and simple animation styles, but timing and storytelling skills take practice. Semifinalists then present the animation and explain their portfolio in ten minutes.",
        time: "Preparation takes weeks. Teams usually sketch the storyboard first, create the artwork and audio, animate and edit, and then write the portfolio sections before the mid-May deadline. Animation is slow work because even short scenes take many frames or steps, so planning the length early matters. Semifinalists prepare a ten minute presentation.",
        cost: "Costs are low because free animation tools exist and the rules ban purchased assets anyway. You mainly need a computer and time. A drawing tablet is optional, and school computer labs can help with software.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who enjoy drawing, animation, and explaining science visually. It suits creative teams of two or more who can split storyboarding, art, animation, and sound. Students interested in digital media will build great skills. Beginners can join with free tutorials."
    },
    "ms-structural-engineering": {
        materials: "Teams bring a pre-built structure made by the team during the current school year, a full-size three-view drawing (front, top, and right end), a cut parts list, and a signed Team Verification form from the chapter advisor. For the semifinal onsite build, TSA issues the planning supplies and a packet of construction materials, and you bring your own toolbox of up to 20 by 10 by 10 inches. The toolbox needs a cutting surface (required), safety glasses with side shields, wax paper of at least 11 by 17 inches, and suggested items such as non-electric cutting tools, adhesives (no aerosol or electric applicators), rulers, abrasives, pliers, and marking tools. Safety eyewear is required onsite.",
        difficulty: "The event measures how efficiently your structure holds weight, so you have to understand how forces move through a design and build precisely. Both the pre-built structure and the onsite structure are destructively tested, and the semifinal asks you to design and build a new structure from supplied materials in three hours. Beginners can learn from simple truss designs, but building and testing experience clearly helps.",
        time: "Preparation is a cycle of research, designing, building, and testing structures to failure so you can improve the next one. Teams often need several builds before the final entry, plus the drawing and parts list. At the conference your structure is tested, and semifinalists then have three hours for an onsite build. Practicing timed builds with unfamiliar materials is the best preparation for that part.",
        cost: "Costs are moderate, mostly from practice. The structure materials are inexpensive, but each test structure is destroyed, so testing adds up. You also need hand tools, adhesives, measuring tools, a cutting surface, and safety glasses. A school shop can often lend tools.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who love building, testing, and improving designs for strength. It suits a pair of partners who enjoy careful hand work and learning from failures. Students interested in civil engineering will find it a good intro. Beginners are welcome with practice."
    },
    "ms-system-control": {
        materials: "Your team brings its own laptop with hardware and software plus a materials kit, because the problem assumes a kit that can identify, secure, and move objects. The guide expects at least two optical sensors, two touch sensors, two motors, two audio and two light outputs, gears, wheels and axles for a vehicle or conveyor, objects such as balls, blocks, and pegs, and Velcro, tape, and clamps. No cutting devices or power tools may be used, so materials must stay in the form you bring them, and each team gets one electrical outlet, so bring a power strip. You also keep an inventor's log, and no reference materials are allowed.",
        difficulty: "The challenge is solving a surprise industrial-style problem by building a model and writing the program onsite. You get 15 minutes to analyze the problem and two and a half hours to build and program, and any code written before the event leads to disqualification. Judges look at repeatability, functional control, how well the model simulates a real process, and efficient use of materials. Beginners can learn it with a robotics kit, but teams with strong sensor and programming experience do better.",
        time: "Preparation is practice with timed sample problems, such as sorting or conveyor tasks. Teams learn the software, practice building common mechanisms, and rehearse splitting the work among three people. There is no project to submit ahead of time. At the conference the preliminary round has 30 minutes for setup, 15 minutes for analysis, and two and a half hours for the solution.",
        cost: "Costs are tied to the kit. A programmable robotics or control kit with sensors and motors is the main purchase, and the laptop is usually already available. Because you cannot cut materials, you do not need many extra supplies. A school robotics program may lend a kit.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like robotics, sensors, and programming a machine to do a job. It suits teams of three who enjoy fast problem solving and dividing building, coding, and testing. Students interested in automation will like the factory-style problems. Beginners can join with a kit."
    },
    "ms-tech-bowl": {
        materials: "Nothing is built or submitted. The preliminary test is online, so each of the three team members brings a laptop (paper tests are used only if officials decide they are necessary), and a scan-sheet fallback needs two sharpened No. 2 pencils. In the semifinal, teams answer questions in head-to-head oral rounds with a buzzer, and transmitting or recording devices are not allowed in the event area.",
        difficulty: "The challenge is a broad range of TSA and technology knowledge answered quickly. The online test is scored as the average of all three teammates, so every member counts, and the semifinal rewards fast, accurate answers with points lost for wrong ones. Beginners can compete with steady studying, but wide general knowledge helps. Teamwork matters because only a limited set of questions allows discussion.",
        time: "Preparation is studying and practice quizzing rather than building. Teams often study technology standards and TSA facts, take practice quizzes, and run mock buzzer rounds to improve speed. There is no pre-conference submission, and onsite the test takes one hour, with an oral round for the 16 semifinal teams.",
        cost: "Costs are minimal. You need a laptop for the online test and study materials, which are often free.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like trivia, quizzes, and learning about many technology topics. It suits teams of three who study together and enjoy fast, friendly competition. Students interested in anything technical will pick up useful knowledge. Beginners can join and learn with practice."
    },
    "ms-technical-design": {
        materials: "The event is a design problem solved on paper, and prototypes or models are not allowed. You prepare a PDF portfolio that follows the technical design loop, with a problem interpretation, a brainstorming record, at least three hand-drawn solution sketches labeled Solution Option 1, 2, and 3, an engineering drawing of the best solution, and a short evaluation of whether it meets the design brief. You need paper, pencils, and drawing tools, plus a way to photograph or scan the sketches and put them into the PDF. A sample design brief in the guide describes a hand-held paper airplane launcher with size limits and a rubber band as the power source.",
        difficulty: "The skill is the design process itself. You must read a brief carefully, list criteria and constraints, brainstorm with a documented technique, compare options with pros and cons, and then produce a clean engineering drawing. Because no building is allowed, the quality of your thinking and drawing is everything. Beginners can compete well, and you only need to learn to sketch clearly and justify choices.",
        time: "You have 24 hours to solve the problem from the moment it is given, so preparation is practice with sample design briefs. Teams practice quick brainstorming, sketching three options, picking the best, and drawing it neatly. The onsite 24 hour window is generous but still requires planning.",
        cost: "Costs are very low. The main supplies are paper, pencils, drawing tools, and access to a computer or scanner to build the PDF.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like sketching, problem solving, and thinking through design choices. It suits a pair of partners who enjoy engineering without building. Students interested in engineering or product design will like the process. Beginners are welcome."
    },
    "ms-video-game-design": {
        materials: "You need game design tools of your choice, a way to host the game online so judges can open a direct link, and a place to host a short demonstration video. Entries that require a download or an executable file are not judged, and the game must be playable for more than three minutes, interactive, and playable to at least the third level. Your PDF portfolio includes a purpose and market research section, a hand-drawn or digitally sketched storyboard, a link to a demonstration video of up to two minutes, a copyright checklist, and a work log. For the semifinal bring one charged device, since there is no power, and no projection is allowed.",
        difficulty: "Building a complete game that holds attention and works in any browser takes real planning. You need game logic, levels, art, and sound, and you also have to explain the target audience and what makes the game unique. Beginners can use a beginner-friendly game tool, but testing and debugging take time. The semifinal gives five minutes to present and five for questions.",
        time: "Expect weeks of work. Teams plan the concept and storyboard, build and test levels, add art and sound, host the game, record the demo, and write the portfolio before the mid-May deadline. The game must stay playable until the end of the conference, so there are no changes after submission.",
        cost: "Costs are low because many game tools and hosting options are free. Any paid assets must have documented permission, so many teams create their own. A laptop is the main requirement.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who love games and want to build one with a team. It suits groups of two to six who can split coding, art, sound, and testing. Students interested in game design or software will gain real skills. Beginners can start with a simple game."
    },
    "ms-website-design": {
        materials: "You build and launch a website that can be reached online at any time, with at least three linked pages and a page labeled Reference that lists your sources and links to a copyright checklist and work log as PDFs. You can use HTML, CSS, and JavaScript or a framework, but pre-built templates and themes are not allowed. The site has to work in current browsers on a desktop or laptop, and changes after submission lead to disqualification. For the semifinal bring one charged device, since no power or guaranteed WiFi is provided.",
        difficulty: "The challenge is making a polished, original site that answers the annual design brief using good layout and clean coding. Without templates you have to design the pages yourself, and you must explain your choices, such as how the site works across browsers and screen resolutions, in the interview. Beginners can start with basic HTML and CSS, but a team of three to six benefits from at least one confident coder.",
        time: "Expect a multi-week project. Teams plan the design, write the pages, test across browsers, and finish documentation before the mid-May deadline, and nothing can change afterward. Semifinalists then rehearse a five minute presentation and a five minute interview.",
        cost: "Costs are low because free editors and free hosting options are available. A custom domain or paid hosting is optional.",
        bestFor: "Best for students who like web design, coding, and visual layout. It suits teams of three to six who can divide design, coding, writing, and testing. Students interested in web development will gain practical skills. Beginners can start with basic HTML and CSS."
    },
    "ms-cybersecurity": {
        materials: "There is nothing to build, but there are two parts to prepare for. The preliminary test is online, so each participant brings a laptop (paper tests are used only if officials decide they are necessary). Semifinalists give a short slide presentation to a pretend corporate board, using their own laptop with an HDMI connection or adapter, and no audio and no power are provided. Real company names and copyrighted material cannot be used, so you invent fictitious companies and use images that are labeled for reuse and cited. The guide's resource list points to practice sites such as TryHackMe, Hack The Box, and CISA's learning pages.",
        difficulty: "The test covers general cybersecurity vocabulary and tasks done by security professionals, so you need to know terms and concepts. The semifinal asks you to identify and explain a security problem, show how physical and digital measures protect information, support your claims with real-world data, and explain trade-offs in just three minutes. Beginners can learn the vocabulary, but connecting ideas to real examples takes practice.",
        time: "Preparation is studying cybersecurity basics for the test and building a short presentation on the yearly problem. Teams practice delivering the three minute talk and answering two minutes of questions. There is no project to submit ahead of time, and the test is one hour onsite.",
        cost: "Costs are very low. Free learning sites cover most study needs, and you only need a laptop.",
        bestFor: "Best for students curious about how to keep computers and data safe. It suits students who enjoy learning technical vocabulary and then explaining a problem and solution clearly. Students thinking about cybersecurity or IT careers will get a useful introduction. Beginners are welcome."
    }
};


export function getEventGuidance(event) {
    return (event && EVENT_GUIDANCE[event.id]) || null;
}
